[{:db/id 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04" :question/id #uuid "1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-342578" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=8x-{x}^{4}$ and the point $P(1,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(7,1)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =8x-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =8-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3719cd77-cf8e-5efe-870f-d9b67a741f79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3719cd77-cf8e-5efe-870f-d9b67a741f79" :answer-field/id #uuid "3719cd77-cf8e-5efe-870f-d9b67a741f79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804" :answer/id #uuid "1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3" :question/id #uuid "513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342598" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=2,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{(-1)}{5} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be729943-ac78-5a58-a4af-33ce85ae0d28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be729943-ac78-5a58-a4af-33ce85ae0d28" :answer-field/id #uuid "be729943-ac78-5a58-a4af-33ce85ae0d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c" :answer/id #uuid "ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-895899f5-e0e0-50f3-bd94-fa13c6acacef" :activity/id #uuid "895899f5-e0e0-50f3-bd94-fa13c6acacef" :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048441 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" "step-455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" "step-975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" "step-acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" "step-d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841"] :activity/first-step "step-a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" :step/id #uuid "a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" :step/content 17592186247103 :step/next "step-455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" :step/id #uuid "455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" :step/content 17592186247110 :step/next "step-975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" :step/id #uuid "975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" :step/content "question-1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04" :step/next "step-acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" :step/id #uuid "acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" :step/content "question-513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3" :step/next "step-d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841" :step/id #uuid "d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841" :step/content "question-70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100937 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" :question/id #uuid "821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-351917" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{1}{2})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27bd1c0c-89e6-510d-8840-4d12f9be8883" "answer-field-a6590ac0-071c-5dc7-835a-5e92e0475c4a" "answer-field-c1127e0f-95b4-5a48-b495-9387fefcb90d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27bd1c0c-89e6-510d-8840-4d12f9be8883" :answer-field/id #uuid "27bd1c0c-89e6-510d-8840-4d12f9be8883" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/id #uuid "d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6590ac0-071c-5dc7-835a-5e92e0475c4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6590ac0-071c-5dc7-835a-5e92e0475c4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/id #uuid "6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1127e0f-95b4-5a48-b495-9387fefcb90d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1127e0f-95b4-5a48-b495-9387fefcb90d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/id #uuid "e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" :question/id #uuid "dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" :question/math-academy-id "q-351931" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{27})}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99bf3307-f793-5f62-81bd-770491a15c81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99bf3307-f793-5f62-81bd-770491a15c81" :answer-field/id #uuid "99bf3307-f793-5f62-81bd-770491a15c81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/id #uuid "60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bd30fdbc-f069-53b3-b51b-30201713a920" :activity/id #uuid "bd30fdbc-f069-53b3-b51b-30201713a920" :activity/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049019 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-7e93dfe2-b7b4-51a0-ae97-f197358d92d1" "step-e48a098e-29f2-5ba7-b16d-7a7a7ab50f0e" "step-9e0b0b1f-b7bd-5ad1-beb2-38585f99a953" "step-ded5f7df-6b0d-5e7d-b05b-669c4c5cc7d9" "step-72480b1b-c0e7-5d75-9725-3eb6bbb0790c"] :activity/first-step "step-7e93dfe2-b7b4-51a0-ae97-f197358d92d1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7e93dfe2-b7b4-51a0-ae97-f197358d92d1" :step/id #uuid "7e93dfe2-b7b4-51a0-ae97-f197358d92d1" :step/content 17592186275347 :step/next "step-e48a098e-29f2-5ba7-b16d-7a7a7ab50f0e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e48a098e-29f2-5ba7-b16d-7a7a7ab50f0e" :step/id #uuid "e48a098e-29f2-5ba7-b16d-7a7a7ab50f0e" :step/content 17592186259780 :step/next "step-9e0b0b1f-b7bd-5ad1-beb2-38585f99a953" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9e0b0b1f-b7bd-5ad1-beb2-38585f99a953" :step/id #uuid "9e0b0b1f-b7bd-5ad1-beb2-38585f99a953" :step/content "question-821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" :step/next "step-ded5f7df-6b0d-5e7d-b05b-669c4c5cc7d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ded5f7df-6b0d-5e7d-b05b-669c4c5cc7d9" :step/id #uuid "ded5f7df-6b0d-5e7d-b05b-669c4c5cc7d9" :step/content 17592186377562 :step/next "step-72480b1b-c0e7-5d75-9725-3eb6bbb0790c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-72480b1b-c0e7-5d75-9725-3eb6bbb0790c" :step/id #uuid "72480b1b-c0e7-5d75-9725-3eb6bbb0790c" :step/content "question-dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c768058-b351-51d4-a894-a96f2a63f5a2" "question-4c942870-19a7-5088-91ca-fc84364e3087"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c768058-b351-51d4-a894-a96f2a63f5a2" :question/id #uuid "2c768058-b351-51d4-a894-a96f2a63f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105639" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (3F+2G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (3F+2G) & =∇\\cdot (3F)+∇\\cdot (2G) \\\\  & =3∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (3F+2G) & =3∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))+2\\cdot (3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})+6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ffd1f69-02c8-5ee4-adf9-edd55635cbea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ffd1f69-02c8-5ee4-adf9-edd55635cbea" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ffd1f69-02c8-5ee4-adf9-edd55635cbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" "answer-54b3d62c-789b-5344-8eb8-1b87f779cb9f" "answer-e85c3dd7-de42-550d-b590-57c43fa3e773" "answer-4665da7c-47c6-56a6-8a63-2cd951c60e91" "answer-04afe455-673f-5b0e-a6fd-b296db94bc7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" :answer/id #uuid "da097bd7-2039-56d9-b3a9-5eda69cefcfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b3d62c-789b-5344-8eb8-1b87f779cb9f" :answer/id #uuid "54b3d62c-789b-5344-8eb8-1b87f779cb9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e85c3dd7-de42-550d-b590-57c43fa3e773" :answer/id #uuid "e85c3dd7-de42-550d-b590-57c43fa3e773" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-12y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4665da7c-47c6-56a6-8a63-2cd951c60e91" :answer/id #uuid "4665da7c-47c6-56a6-8a63-2cd951c60e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04afe455-673f-5b0e-a6fd-b296db94bc7e" :answer/id #uuid "04afe455-673f-5b0e-a6fd-b296db94bc7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37458a2a-1dd8-5f4b-97d7-084609fd0a20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c942870-19a7-5088-91ca-fc84364e3087" :question/id #uuid "4c942870-19a7-5088-91ca-fc84364e3087" :question/math-academy-id "q-131243" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (-4F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF={x}^{2}yz\\,i+({y}^{3}-y)\\,j-xy{z}^{2}\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-4F)=-4∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xyz+3{y}^{2}-1-2xyz \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-4F) & =-4∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (3{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-12{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc2ee70c-3ea3-5fe5-8d90-cd968896e62f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc2ee70c-3ea3-5fe5-8d90-cd968896e62f" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2ee70c-3ea3-5fe5-8d90-cd968896e62f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40bcbcfd-b0a9-5f9e-b620-2401616f5850" "answer-f9c811ea-cec4-5a24-807f-3cb9271e236c" "answer-321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" "answer-51f59a6c-efd5-5c01-9c0b-9478a549ed93" "answer-558eb743-ed39-5b77-8c92-39d8417ede55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40bcbcfd-b0a9-5f9e-b620-2401616f5850" :answer/id #uuid "40bcbcfd-b0a9-5f9e-b620-2401616f5850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1-12{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9c811ea-cec4-5a24-807f-3cb9271e236c" :answer/id #uuid "f9c811ea-cec4-5a24-807f-3cb9271e236c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16xyz-12{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" :answer/id #uuid "321ce1f2-898d-5430-9186-5d15072c22b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4-12{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51f59a6c-efd5-5c01-9c0b-9478a549ed93" :answer/id #uuid "51f59a6c-efd5-5c01-9c0b-9478a549ed93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xyz+{y}^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-558eb743-ed39-5b77-8c92-39d8417ede55" :answer/id #uuid "558eb743-ed39-5b77-8c92-39d8417ede55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37458a2a-1dd8-5f4b-97d7-084609fd0a20" :question/id #uuid "37458a2a-1dd8-5f4b-97d7-084609fd0a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-131291" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x{z}^{3},{z}^{2}⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2xz,0,{x}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})=\\frac{1}{x}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z(\\frac{1}{x}+2z)+⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,x{z}^{3},{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,0,{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =xz+2{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =xz+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =xz(1+3xz+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ba8f018-adaf-5841-94d1-797bd241ddf9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ba8f018-adaf-5841-94d1-797bd241ddf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba8f018-adaf-5841-94d1-797bd241ddf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" "answer-5670544a-efa4-5dea-bb60-d1317fd8ae11" "answer-b1f47d0b-4c4f-560a-896d-281726884e02" "answer-4441e299-9c2b-5890-9b2f-cf910acd93da" "answer-39889727-a276-5682-8ef7-8911020878d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" :answer/id #uuid "c2a78d89-bcff-52b4-a64b-a195e189c0f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz(1+3xz+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5670544a-efa4-5dea-bb60-d1317fd8ae11" :answer/id #uuid "5670544a-efa4-5dea-bb60-d1317fd8ae11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz(1+3yxz+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1f47d0b-4c4f-560a-896d-281726884e02" :answer/id #uuid "b1f47d0b-4c4f-560a-896d-281726884e02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz(1+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}+2xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4441e299-9c2b-5890-9b2f-cf910acd93da" :answer/id #uuid "4441e299-9c2b-5890-9b2f-cf910acd93da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz(1+xz+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39889727-a276-5682-8ef7-8911020878d8" :answer/id #uuid "39889727-a276-5682-8ef7-8911020878d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xz(3xz+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-43cd0e68-5cb8-572a-ba2e-ef8b0404ffac" :activity/id #uuid "43cd0e68-5cb8-572a-ba2e-ef8b0404ffac" :activity/title "Properties of the Divergence Operator" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049765 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-5eb56b94-7439-5882-b527-53d920358a26" "step-a7f009b6-e8bd-5ad5-a7ce-b0ae93ab904e" "step-0017b014-af13-5dd7-825c-45a467efd98c" "step-cff4c98e-4515-5b93-aac2-f02400e27f42" "step-2ef3bb86-4410-5f70-b715-2125b5cff05f"] :activity/first-step "step-5eb56b94-7439-5882-b527-53d920358a26" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-5eb56b94-7439-5882-b527-53d920358a26" :step/id #uuid "5eb56b94-7439-5882-b527-53d920358a26" :step/content 17592186179909 :step/next "step-a7f009b6-e8bd-5ad5-a7ce-b0ae93ab904e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a7f009b6-e8bd-5ad5-a7ce-b0ae93ab904e" :step/id #uuid "a7f009b6-e8bd-5ad5-a7ce-b0ae93ab904e" :step/content "question-2c768058-b351-51d4-a894-a96f2a63f5a2" :step/next "step-0017b014-af13-5dd7-825c-45a467efd98c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0017b014-af13-5dd7-825c-45a467efd98c" :step/id #uuid "0017b014-af13-5dd7-825c-45a467efd98c" :step/content 17592186366829 :step/next "step-cff4c98e-4515-5b93-aac2-f02400e27f42" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cff4c98e-4515-5b93-aac2-f02400e27f42" :step/id #uuid "cff4c98e-4515-5b93-aac2-f02400e27f42" :step/content "question-4c942870-19a7-5088-91ca-fc84364e3087" :step/next "step-2ef3bb86-4410-5f70-b715-2125b5cff05f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ef3bb86-4410-5f70-b715-2125b5cff05f" :step/id #uuid "2ef3bb86-4410-5f70-b715-2125b5cff05f" :step/content "question-37458a2a-1dd8-5f4b-97d7-084609fd0a20" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ebdd2a76-ec62-5920-99df-a78d7b00016a" "question-2f01ee13-fe5d-5b17-b506-bfc6a72b2888"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105525 :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB},$ we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x-{10}^{∘})+(x+{20}^{∘})+(x-{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =x-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m\\angle A = 50^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105739 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-02e57395-d812-5a19-a50a-4f3093174d3b" "question-c42a06c6-86aa-5826-a78d-ab4a4cc2e08a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105526 :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HFG+m∠EFH & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG+{105}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG & ={75}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠G+m∠HFG+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {75}^{∘}+{75}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {150}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105740 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d3dcdfc-6059-5bce-b1ec-1f29a3baad3b" "question-cfe6f4b2-4744-5b79-aa1c-bad69130f89a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105584 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =AC \\\\ 4x-3 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =x+4 \\\\  & =(2)+4 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1cbf3ae4-b692-5171-8065-1b037cf779e6" "question-ee71d401-4421-55e1-931e-7e917f7688f0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105527 :question/problem "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle{D}?$\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD & =m∠CAD-m∠BAC \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD+m∠D+m∠ABD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠BAD+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {40}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {80}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebdd2a76-ec62-5920-99df-a78d7b00016a" :question/id #uuid "ebdd2a76-ec62-5920-99df-a78d7b00016a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74856" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbc4368e30cfd0543d085fc8baa644031b35f4086f2da53d5aaecf6b4b626245.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠B$ in the triangle  $△ABC$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{CB},$ we conclude that $△ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=2x-{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (2x-{9}^{∘})+(2x-{9}^{∘})+(4x+{14}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 8x-{4}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={184}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={23}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x={23}^{∘}$ into the equation for the measure of $∠B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =m∠A \\\\  & =2x-{9}^{∘} \\\\  & =2({23}^{∘})-{9}^{∘} \\\\  & ={37}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m∠B={37}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0fb07700-e768-5a8e-8424-3dc71e068bd6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0fb07700-e768-5a8e-8424-3dc71e068bd6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb07700-e768-5a8e-8424-3dc71e068bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-569b9ce0-1473-55bc-a6de-af288bdec9b1" "answer-3c9a5fcf-b34a-548a-b949-345148f543b6" "answer-1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" "answer-6decc0f1-591f-5edf-b443-7609212d6497" "answer-808b0c6e-d096-5504-9924-734f0194e469"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-569b9ce0-1473-55bc-a6de-af288bdec9b1" :answer/id #uuid "569b9ce0-1473-55bc-a6de-af288bdec9b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{34}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c9a5fcf-b34a-548a-b949-345148f543b6" :answer/id #uuid "3c9a5fcf-b34a-548a-b949-345148f543b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" :answer/id #uuid "1ab71926-9e86-5df1-a1bb-fd736c9137be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{37}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6decc0f1-591f-5edf-b443-7609212d6497" :answer/id #uuid "6decc0f1-591f-5edf-b443-7609212d6497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-808b0c6e-d096-5504-9924-734f0194e469" :answer/id #uuid "808b0c6e-d096-5504-9924-734f0194e469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f01ee13-fe5d-5b17-b506-bfc6a72b2888" :question/id #uuid "2f01ee13-fe5d-5b17-b506-bfc6a72b2888" :question/math-academy-id "q-118661" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4da332c74f255825ae0073ca41117ae477f72262ef8d45911b9ce48ed3b8ccf0.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠G$ in the triangle $△FGH$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overset{―}{FG}≅\\overset{―}{GH},$ we conclude that $△FGH$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠F=m∠H=x+{13}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠F+m∠G+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{13}^{∘})+(2x+{18}^{∘})+(x+{13}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x+{44}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={136}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={34}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x={34}^{∘}$ into the equation for the measure of $∠G$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠G & =2x+{18}^{∘} \\\\  & =2({34}^{∘})+{18}^{∘} \\\\  & ={86}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m∠G={86}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ffd31e8b-b3ec-560a-b1c8-c443d4f6582d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ffd31e8b-b3ec-560a-b1c8-c443d4f6582d" :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd31e8b-b3ec-560a-b1c8-c443d4f6582d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a37cf3c-005f-5a4b-aeac-abaf78c3a7d7" "answer-5d9e6a70-c971-5bac-8559-77499b986da7" "answer-dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" "answer-02691191-a222-5e50-b003-388e2c6df4a6" "answer-9b7ccc92-79c7-5431-a394-1f8287934666"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a37cf3c-005f-5a4b-aeac-abaf78c3a7d7" :answer/id #uuid "1a37cf3c-005f-5a4b-aeac-abaf78c3a7d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{83}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9e6a70-c971-5bac-8559-77499b986da7" :answer/id #uuid "5d9e6a70-c971-5bac-8559-77499b986da7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{85}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" :answer/id #uuid "dc9bb2da-7645-5f43-9a6e-24089049974f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02691191-a222-5e50-b003-388e2c6df4a6" :answer/id #uuid "02691191-a222-5e50-b003-388e2c6df4a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{87}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b7ccc92-79c7-5431-a394-1f8287934666" :answer/id #uuid "9b7ccc92-79c7-5431-a394-1f8287934666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{89}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02e57395-d812-5a19-a50a-4f3093174d3b" :question/id #uuid "02e57395-d812-5a19-a50a-4f3093174d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171893" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb0478d757190db93eeccddda17f122be97230308655eeb0983add2a316866a5.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, the value of $y$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $∠BCA$ and $∠BCD$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BCA+m∠BCD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA+y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘}-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{BC}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm∠B=m∠A={56}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {56}^{∘}+{56}^{∘}+({180}^{∘}-y) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ y & ={112}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bca22090-6107-5000-a622-c53df9beee48"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bca22090-6107-5000-a622-c53df9beee48" :answer-field/id #uuid "bca22090-6107-5000-a622-c53df9beee48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13" :answer/id #uuid "06b297e5-0321-5cb8-ba70-a7357cf14e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c42a06c6-86aa-5826-a78d-ab4a4cc2e08a" :question/id #uuid "c42a06c6-86aa-5826-a78d-ab4a4cc2e08a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118675" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4600221042deaad471de628179200fa62872fdfe38220ca66eaded966f8894e.png)\n\nFind the value of $w$ in the triangle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $∠RTS$ and $∠STU$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RTS+m∠STU & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RTS+w & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠RTS & ={180}^{∘}-w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $△RST$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overset{―}{RT}≅\\overset{―}{TS}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm∠S=m∠R={39}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠RTS & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {39}^{∘}+{39}^{∘}+({180}^{∘}-w) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {39}^{∘}+{39}^{∘}-w & =0 \\\\ w & ={78}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afe57a34-7063-5007-9fab-e9e8d3248327"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afe57a34-7063-5007-9fab-e9e8d3248327" :answer-field/id #uuid "afe57a34-7063-5007-9fab-e9e8d3248327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d468839f-1657-5c4f-b5b6-5799add32068" "answer-bf1508d2-3625-57e0-8cdc-fb91f4d4dd82" "answer-a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" "answer-dfff6e7d-f412-5719-8768-9faaec5eed7b" "answer-b06b543d-07f0-55f8-b6aa-b4c57d998ca9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d468839f-1657-5c4f-b5b6-5799add32068" :answer/id #uuid "d468839f-1657-5c4f-b5b6-5799add32068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf1508d2-3625-57e0-8cdc-fb91f4d4dd82" :answer/id #uuid "bf1508d2-3625-57e0-8cdc-fb91f4d4dd82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{77}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" :answer/id #uuid "a1723209-b261-53ec-9c92-067b43c991cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{78}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfff6e7d-f412-5719-8768-9faaec5eed7b" :answer/id #uuid "dfff6e7d-f412-5719-8768-9faaec5eed7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b06b543d-07f0-55f8-b6aa-b4c57d998ca9" :answer/id #uuid "b06b543d-07f0-55f8-b6aa-b4c57d998ca9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d3dcdfc-6059-5bce-b1ec-1f29a3baad3b" :question/id #uuid "9d3dcdfc-6059-5bce-b1ec-1f29a3baad3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-68286" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c182b73af6ff37c3c673fe0e174e8f86e2f89a0d38ed385f64e8b8b3a4731fc8.png)\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ shown above is isosceles with base $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ What is the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =AC \\\\ 3x-3 & =2x+2 \\\\ x & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x-4 \\\\  & =2(5)-4 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0df4cd81-2779-55e2-9b8a-f7cdb56d9688"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0df4cd81-2779-55e2-9b8a-f7cdb56d9688" :answer-field/id #uuid "0df4cd81-2779-55e2-9b8a-f7cdb56d9688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34d7776e-9158-50f7-8089-722db7df5376" "answer-33789998-29f6-5249-a338-28872495f28c" "answer-d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" "answer-76d9d46a-138d-5b6e-9d55-b7e14c6c27de" "answer-c409a004-37c0-5a78-9094-3b282436335a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34d7776e-9158-50f7-8089-722db7df5376" :answer/id #uuid "34d7776e-9158-50f7-8089-722db7df5376" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33789998-29f6-5249-a338-28872495f28c" :answer/id #uuid "33789998-29f6-5249-a338-28872495f28c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" :answer/id #uuid "d3ab559c-b189-5f62-96a0-04db72b15a12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76d9d46a-138d-5b6e-9d55-b7e14c6c27de" :answer/id #uuid "76d9d46a-138d-5b6e-9d55-b7e14c6c27de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c409a004-37c0-5a78-9094-3b282436335a" :answer/id #uuid "c409a004-37c0-5a78-9094-3b282436335a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cfe6f4b2-4744-5b79-aa1c-bad69130f89a" :question/id #uuid "cfe6f4b2-4744-5b79-aa1c-bad69130f89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118817" :question/problem "![](images/87/876a5edd5e10017ffb5f858481294d121b0cfec87e936538ffde0ceb9af5c663.png)\n\nThe triangle $△TUV$ shown above is isosceles with the base $\\overset{―}{TV}.$ What is the perimeter of $△TUV?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $△TUV$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{TV}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{TU}≅\\overset{―}{UV}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =UV \\\\ 5x-8 & =4x+3 \\\\ x & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe perimeter of $△TUV$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =TU+UV+TV \\\\  & =(5x-8)+(4x+3)+(2x+5) \\\\  & =11x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=11$ in the expression for the perimeter, we get\n\n$$\np=11x=11(11)=121.\n$$\n\nHence, $p=121.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3723024c-cd1f-50ee-aa66-c7cfc430c8a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3723024c-cd1f-50ee-aa66-c7cfc430c8a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "3723024c-cd1f-50ee-aa66-c7cfc430c8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f248e42-272d-5cb8-ad97-86dcebabdb4d" "answer-3a3430dd-2a88-5cfd-94e2-4ef72aa0f912" "answer-4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" "answer-bb559f99-484b-51d7-968a-ce70bc8be11e" "answer-3c0a90fc-dbab-59c4-90bb-f10642d66abe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f248e42-272d-5cb8-ad97-86dcebabdb4d" :answer/id #uuid "5f248e42-272d-5cb8-ad97-86dcebabdb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "110" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a3430dd-2a88-5cfd-94e2-4ef72aa0f912" :answer/id #uuid "3a3430dd-2a88-5cfd-94e2-4ef72aa0f912" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "111" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" :answer/id #uuid "4ea46e62-57fa-5e6a-8a1d-b3bc4a07e653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "121" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb559f99-484b-51d7-968a-ce70bc8be11e" :answer/id #uuid "bb559f99-484b-51d7-968a-ce70bc8be11e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "134" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c0a90fc-dbab-59c4-90bb-f10642d66abe" :answer/id #uuid "3c0a90fc-dbab-59c4-90bb-f10642d66abe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1cbf3ae4-b692-5171-8065-1b037cf779e6" :question/id #uuid "1cbf3ae4-b692-5171-8065-1b037cf779e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-171896" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbc303a7d1ee4bcad3bdbd29b4897e972556bfe89bdff2831aaeef642026b98b.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the measure of $∠D$ is equal to{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle. Consequently, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠ABC={72}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△CDB$ is an isosceles triangle, we have:\n\n$$\nm∠D=m∠BCD\n$$\n\nSince $∠ABC$ and $∠CBD$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ABC+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {72}^{∘}+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠CBD & ={108}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $m∠D$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠D+m∠BCD+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D+m∠D+{108}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠D & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={36}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b91626e3-0a70-5ba0-81e2-883eb4b5223c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b91626e3-0a70-5ba0-81e2-883eb4b5223c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b91626e3-0a70-5ba0-81e2-883eb4b5223c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861" :answer/id #uuid "ef6fa6a3-2eab-5526-b415-5da01b5cf861" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee71d401-4421-55e1-931e-7e917f7688f0" :question/id #uuid "ee71d401-4421-55e1-931e-7e917f7688f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118950" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcd036443bb904abafd7ad0cc2aea6ead68538835c08d8b99958291f393a4476.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $∠S$ above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $△PQR$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to ${60}^{∘}:$ \n\n$$\nm∠P=m∠PQR=m∠QRP={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△QRS$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠RQS=m∠S\n$$\n\n![](images/79/792155c685456f6c278fff5a0341f67358a8b0983d696cf55a6867c9c71ec7cb.png)\n\nNotice also that $∠QRP$, $∠SRQ$ and $∠TRS$ are supplementary, therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRP+m∠SRQ+m∠TRS & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {60}^{∘}+m∠SRQ+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠SRQ+{130}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠SRQ & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $△QRS$, using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RQS+m∠S+m∠SRQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠S+m∠S+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠S & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ m∠S & ={65}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76b96617-6d7f-5cdb-9d08-1c3ef885522b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76b96617-6d7f-5cdb-9d08-1c3ef885522b" :answer-field/id #uuid "76b96617-6d7f-5cdb-9d08-1c3ef885522b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" :answer-field/choices ["answer-947119b6-be54-5bf8-8fea-6fdf1288eb43" "answer-ccb7d431-9e21-5085-822a-5db344f8a02a" "answer-ed99da56-c6d1-5ad1-a054-b48dc6be15dd" "answer-94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" "answer-8c0abbb6-f8b6-5629-8bf7-efbca658aa53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-947119b6-be54-5bf8-8fea-6fdf1288eb43" :answer/id #uuid "947119b6-be54-5bf8-8fea-6fdf1288eb43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccb7d431-9e21-5085-822a-5db344f8a02a" :answer/id #uuid "ccb7d431-9e21-5085-822a-5db344f8a02a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed99da56-c6d1-5ad1-a054-b48dc6be15dd" :answer/id #uuid "ed99da56-c6d1-5ad1-a054-b48dc6be15dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" :answer/id #uuid "94ca3912-e9df-5aad-9f35-f1dfc7eb6279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c0abbb6-f8b6-5629-8bf7-efbca658aa53" :answer/id #uuid "8c0abbb6-f8b6-5629-8bf7-efbca658aa53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{67}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107043 :tutorial/content "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:\n\n*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: \n\n*If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106021 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-31c1a37a-969b-5cd4-b27b-164fe43832ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-31c1a37a-969b-5cd4-b27b-164fe43832ff" :question/id #uuid "31c1a37a-969b-5cd4-b27b-164fe43832ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-38110" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z\\}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n\nII. If $Tx=[1,-2,3]^{T}$ has no solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nIII. If $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\}$, then $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z,$ any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution, this means that $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$ Therefore, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\}=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6e1f623-bd44-5493-8260-283c244d07a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6e1f623-bd44-5493-8260-283c244d07a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e1f623-bd44-5493-8260-283c244d07a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf539e0c-e708-5741-a821-693e7816c138" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80cebb06-f523-5475-be03-9796f40d9119" "answer-bf539e0c-e708-5741-a821-693e7816c138" "answer-972cf002-819d-58f7-b85f-fbe6af417f69" "answer-45611dda-2e80-5e17-ad02-20bf7af10032" "answer-d2715267-331a-5a1d-8909-dea7f731f442"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80cebb06-f523-5475-be03-9796f40d9119" :answer/id #uuid "80cebb06-f523-5475-be03-9796f40d9119" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf539e0c-e708-5741-a821-693e7816c138" :answer/id #uuid "bf539e0c-e708-5741-a821-693e7816c138" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-972cf002-819d-58f7-b85f-fbe6af417f69" :answer/id #uuid "972cf002-819d-58f7-b85f-fbe6af417f69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45611dda-2e80-5e17-ad02-20bf7af10032" :answer/id #uuid "45611dda-2e80-5e17-ad02-20bf7af10032" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2715267-331a-5a1d-8909-dea7f731f442" :answer/id #uuid "d2715267-331a-5a1d-8909-dea7f731f442" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366441 :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z,w]^{T}\\,:\\,x=2z,\\,\\,y=w\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2z \\\\ w \\\\ z \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+w{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186406840 :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186365087 :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x-y+2z=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=x+2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x+2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-64479ace-7012-51d4-b2b4-8738e61571ac" :activity/id #uuid "64479ace-7012-51d4-b2b4-8738e61571ac" :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049880 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-58501b2a-32e9-5f10-a02d-62409c76c2b9" "step-29152df7-9145-56fd-81cf-7990f5f65284" "step-1c8b644d-c1aa-5054-80bb-2094a58661c1" "step-7cd92d7a-8193-53d1-bf92-9e1fa1598872" "step-04cb0c84-a155-5f7d-84eb-582ddec2e63e"] :activity/first-step "step-58501b2a-32e9-5f10-a02d-62409c76c2b9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-58501b2a-32e9-5f10-a02d-62409c76c2b9" :step/id #uuid "58501b2a-32e9-5f10-a02d-62409c76c2b9" :step/content "question-31c1a37a-969b-5cd4-b27b-164fe43832ff" :step/next "step-29152df7-9145-56fd-81cf-7990f5f65284" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-29152df7-9145-56fd-81cf-7990f5f65284" :step/id #uuid "29152df7-9145-56fd-81cf-7990f5f65284" :step/content 17592186366427 :step/next "step-1c8b644d-c1aa-5054-80bb-2094a58661c1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1c8b644d-c1aa-5054-80bb-2094a58661c1" :step/id #uuid "1c8b644d-c1aa-5054-80bb-2094a58661c1" :step/content 17592186366441 :step/next "step-7cd92d7a-8193-53d1-bf92-9e1fa1598872" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7cd92d7a-8193-53d1-bf92-9e1fa1598872" :step/id #uuid "7cd92d7a-8193-53d1-bf92-9e1fa1598872" :step/content 17592186406840 :step/next "step-04cb0c84-a155-5f7d-84eb-582ddec2e63e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-04cb0c84-a155-5f7d-84eb-582ddec2e63e" :step/id #uuid "04cb0c84-a155-5f7d-84eb-582ddec2e63e" :step/content 17592186365087 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields 17592186195332] [:db/retract 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields 17592186195312] [:db/retract 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields 17592186195317] [:db/retract 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields 17592186195322] [:db/retract 17592186198772 :question/answer-fields 17592186195327] {:db/id 17592186198772 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d550ecec3b7103c9510b6e45e738ae1385e07fa63ff2e50c54da6c17606faaec.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in \\{-3,-2,-1\\},\\,y\\in \\{-4,-3,-2,-1\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198793 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f95f6b259237816a5a3da041452cfd9458f0852b1ae717a044738b6f310ab5d.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2x+3\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3$ and *below* the line $y=2x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3,\\,y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3<y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=3$ and $y=2x+3$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\le y\\le 2x+3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le y\\le 2x+3\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d22f4f49-3d24-597d-a691-de6244dc4cd7" :activity/id #uuid "d22f4f49-3d24-597d-a691-de6244dc4cd7" :activity/title "Describing Planar Regions Using Set-Builder Notation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051063 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-50aad182-df62-5dbf-9c9d-2ec59878210d" "step-c0bdaf39-9d92-5700-8ede-9e1aa028df66" "step-e9a5c0e4-438b-5946-9925-7c8c06465e28" "step-caa8acd9-52f7-5d1b-ad76-70a084b67f2a" "step-47e338fb-f8e6-5ac1-bdf5-13782bbced45"] :activity/first-step "step-50aad182-df62-5dbf-9c9d-2ec59878210d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-50aad182-df62-5dbf-9c9d-2ec59878210d" :step/id #uuid "50aad182-df62-5dbf-9c9d-2ec59878210d" :step/content 17592186198772 :step/next "step-c0bdaf39-9d92-5700-8ede-9e1aa028df66" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c0bdaf39-9d92-5700-8ede-9e1aa028df66" :step/id #uuid "c0bdaf39-9d92-5700-8ede-9e1aa028df66" :step/content 17592186198793 :step/next "step-e9a5c0e4-438b-5946-9925-7c8c06465e28" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9a5c0e4-438b-5946-9925-7c8c06465e28" :step/id #uuid "e9a5c0e4-438b-5946-9925-7c8c06465e28" :step/content 17592186287725 :step/next "step-caa8acd9-52f7-5d1b-ad76-70a084b67f2a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-caa8acd9-52f7-5d1b-ad76-70a084b67f2a" :step/id #uuid "caa8acd9-52f7-5d1b-ad76-70a084b67f2a" :step/content 17592186374485 :step/next "step-47e338fb-f8e6-5ac1-bdf5-13782bbced45" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-47e338fb-f8e6-5ac1-bdf5-13782bbced45" :step/id #uuid "47e338fb-f8e6-5ac1-bdf5-13782bbced45" :step/content 17592186287798 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-151f16ce-dba7-51c2-a380-fc347234ba8a" "question-fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" "question-8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" "question-1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" "question-854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092136 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\arsinh{2} =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\arsinh x$ is \n\n$$\n\\arsinh{x} =\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\arsinh{2} & = \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{2^2+1}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   \n& = \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{4+1}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n&=\\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt 5\\right).\n\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092290 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" "question-825bc5fd-74d7-5ef1-94a9-eb5175f0a88c" "question-90b49ff0-e80a-5bed-95c6-624948fee870" "question-7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" "question-3e112ff9-57de-5d36-99af-b8be46637221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092137 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\arcosh{3} =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\arcosh{x}$ is \n\n$$\n\\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\arcosh{3}\n                                                       \n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{3^2-1}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   \n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{9-1}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{8}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n&=\\ln{\\left(3+2\\sqrt{2}\\right)}.\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" "question-c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" "question-83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" "question-b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" "question-670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092138 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\artanh\\left ( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right ) =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\artanh{x}$ is \n\n$$\n\\artanh{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\artanh\\left ( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right )\n                \n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\dfrac{1}{2}}{1-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{\\dfrac{3}{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln{3}.\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" "question-2480f399-e4b8-57fb-80b2-6dd5de7e6195" "question-21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" "question-dec2af6f-6d06-5905-b90e-1ab8bd17313c" "question-4f32f1c5-eb40-50cf-b4aa-8b349651b9ca"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092140 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nUsing the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate $f^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x) = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}$ and $x \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet $y = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}.$\nSince $x \\geq 0,$ we have $y \\geq 0.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\sinh{\\left(y^2\\right)},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in[0,\\infty)$ and $y\\in[0,\\infty).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\sinh x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-2x{e}^{{y}^{2}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{{y}^{2}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{{y}^{2}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y = e^{y^2}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{y^2} >0$ for all real $y,$ and $x -\\sqrt{x^2+1} <0,$ we must select the positive branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{y}^{2}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-151f16ce-dba7-51c2-a380-fc347234ba8a" :question/id #uuid "151f16ce-dba7-51c2-a380-fc347234ba8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95603" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b4ce545-2d83-5c19-901d-dad9c902c200"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b4ce545-2d83-5c19-901d-dad9c902c200" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4ce545-2d83-5c19-901d-dad9c902c200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d37af6cc-1771-526f-b7cc-27b1d00efc67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa1db72a-7638-5bc1-ba80-1a764a056da9" "answer-d37af6cc-1771-526f-b7cc-27b1d00efc67" "answer-d218f0d8-1462-5304-87f2-18d25ea2def5" "answer-3f8ddd90-a65c-5c7c-9f4b-c3dff4aa8088" "answer-02f914b7-4ef9-5bba-881b-fc1b5338ca11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa1db72a-7638-5bc1-ba80-1a764a056da9" :answer/id #uuid "aa1db72a-7638-5bc1-ba80-1a764a056da9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d37af6cc-1771-526f-b7cc-27b1d00efc67" :answer/id #uuid "d37af6cc-1771-526f-b7cc-27b1d00efc67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{10})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d218f0d8-1462-5304-87f2-18d25ea2def5" :answer/id #uuid "d218f0d8-1462-5304-87f2-18d25ea2def5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f8ddd90-a65c-5c7c-9f4b-c3dff4aa8088" :answer/id #uuid "3f8ddd90-a65c-5c7c-9f4b-c3dff4aa8088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9+\\sqrt{10})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02f914b7-4ef9-5bba-881b-fc1b5338ca11" :answer/id #uuid "02f914b7-4ef9-5bba-881b-fc1b5338ca11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9+\\sqrt{7})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" :question/id #uuid "fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125843" :question/problem "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" "answer-f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" "answer-210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" "answer-75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" "answer-5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" :answer/id #uuid "42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" :answer/id #uuid "f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" :answer/id #uuid "210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" :answer/id #uuid "75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" :question/id #uuid "8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-125838" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" "answer-0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" "answer-ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" "answer-30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" "answer-9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" :answer/id #uuid "06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" :answer/id #uuid "0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" :answer/id #uuid "ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" :answer/id #uuid "30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" :question/id #uuid "1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" :question/math-academy-id "q-342289" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{m}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd" :answer-field/id #uuid "e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b" :question/id #uuid "854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9601" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7107c43c-bc1f-5f63-9a1c-c16d62962b34"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7107c43c-bc1f-5f63-9a1c-c16d62962b34" :answer-field/id #uuid "7107c43c-bc1f-5f63-9a1c-c16d62962b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70ac23c1-c539-56dc-ab99-2f9ae1aa9224" "answer-c6970587-5e60-5326-ba2f-20db18796990" "answer-64b7ad1d-e8f4-5ece-9cdb-d0b6a18c31ad" "answer-4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" "answer-fbd92dee-655a-57ac-b2c7-90a821947212"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ac23c1-c539-56dc-ab99-2f9ae1aa9224" :answer/id #uuid "70ac23c1-c539-56dc-ab99-2f9ae1aa9224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6970587-5e60-5326-ba2f-20db18796990" :answer/id #uuid "c6970587-5e60-5326-ba2f-20db18796990" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64b7ad1d-e8f4-5ece-9cdb-d0b6a18c31ad" :answer/id #uuid "64b7ad1d-e8f4-5ece-9cdb-d0b6a18c31ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+6}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/id #uuid "4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd92dee-655a-57ac-b2c7-90a821947212" :answer/id #uuid "fbd92dee-655a-57ac-b2c7-90a821947212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/id #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341314" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-825bc5fd-74d7-5ef1-94a9-eb5175f0a88c" :question/id #uuid "825bc5fd-74d7-5ef1-94a9-eb5175f0a88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95612" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $t\\ge 1,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{t}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡{t}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{t}^{4}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-166baeab-8e67-55ec-8521-064948d2e2ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-166baeab-8e67-55ec-8521-064948d2e2ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "166baeab-8e67-55ec-8521-064948d2e2ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7189b48-7aaf-54df-9ab9-f54ac6ef4215" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bab2f251-561a-5061-9968-2a666cadb472" "answer-f32386a5-3a17-5e0b-8e43-dc3ded6055a0" "answer-c7189b48-7aaf-54df-9ab9-f54ac6ef4215" "answer-2285b527-2ab8-5af2-afd1-7f4347e00b37" "answer-7ed05857-49fc-5316-b172-7d379a7d5223"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bab2f251-561a-5061-9968-2a666cadb472" :answer/id #uuid "bab2f251-561a-5061-9968-2a666cadb472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f32386a5-3a17-5e0b-8e43-dc3ded6055a0" :answer/id #uuid "f32386a5-3a17-5e0b-8e43-dc3ded6055a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7189b48-7aaf-54df-9ab9-f54ac6ef4215" :answer/id #uuid "c7189b48-7aaf-54df-9ab9-f54ac6ef4215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+\\sqrt{{t}^{4}-1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2285b527-2ab8-5af2-afd1-7f4347e00b37" :answer/id #uuid "2285b527-2ab8-5af2-afd1-7f4347e00b37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{4}+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ed05857-49fc-5316-b172-7d379a7d5223" :answer/id #uuid "7ed05857-49fc-5316-b172-7d379a7d5223" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{4}+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-90b49ff0-e80a-5bed-95c6-624948fee870" :question/id #uuid "90b49ff0-e80a-5bed-95c6-624948fee870" :question/math-academy-id "q-95624" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{36-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{35}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-70971762-5358-56e1-be1f-c0899c319a2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-70971762-5358-56e1-be1f-c0899c319a2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "70971762-5358-56e1-be1f-c0899c319a2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aa6a95e3-1172-54ed-8fdf-d10d89339f93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-468e30ca-2c3f-55a3-bab1-e10249c2d5c1" "answer-442742ea-5a97-5424-b2fe-831762115423" "answer-897b951e-0a56-57e6-8c94-07c07157d181" "answer-aa6a95e3-1172-54ed-8fdf-d10d89339f93" "answer-c3f5503c-062e-58b9-85e0-9b802c889465"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468e30ca-2c3f-55a3-bab1-e10249c2d5c1" :answer/id #uuid "468e30ca-2c3f-55a3-bab1-e10249c2d5c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(35+\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-442742ea-5a97-5424-b2fe-831762115423" :answer/id #uuid "442742ea-5a97-5424-b2fe-831762115423" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36+\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-897b951e-0a56-57e6-8c94-07c07157d181" :answer/id #uuid "897b951e-0a56-57e6-8c94-07c07157d181" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36-\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa6a95e3-1172-54ed-8fdf-d10d89339f93" :answer/id #uuid "aa6a95e3-1172-54ed-8fdf-d10d89339f93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{35})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3f5503c-062e-58b9-85e0-9b802c889465" :answer/id #uuid "c3f5503c-062e-58b9-85e0-9b802c889465" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{37})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" :question/id #uuid "7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" :question/math-academy-id "q-343590" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $p\\ge 25,$ express $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e112ff9-57de-5d36-99af-b8be46637221" :question/id #uuid "3e112ff9-57de-5d36-99af-b8be46637221" :question/math-academy-id "q-9587" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{12-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b17bfec9-1d4c-50d7-8f82-68de7623b09d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b17bfec9-1d4c-50d7-8f82-68de7623b09d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b17bfec9-1d4c-50d7-8f82-68de7623b09d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de03a752-430a-5753-9efb-66f96ebd0eeb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de03a752-430a-5753-9efb-66f96ebd0eeb" "answer-de16719d-f79b-5efa-bc68-e15a52af71eb" "answer-bc6e8f31-658c-5344-87e7-48293066b0d7" "answer-3ac03210-e5fe-5e87-a1ac-e095d0d6a5ec" "answer-9f1a2af3-553e-59e3-9e85-b6861220aee1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de03a752-430a-5753-9efb-66f96ebd0eeb" :answer/id #uuid "de03a752-430a-5753-9efb-66f96ebd0eeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de16719d-f79b-5efa-bc68-e15a52af71eb" :answer/id #uuid "de16719d-f79b-5efa-bc68-e15a52af71eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc6e8f31-658c-5344-87e7-48293066b0d7" :answer/id #uuid "bc6e8f31-658c-5344-87e7-48293066b0d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{11})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ac03210-e5fe-5e87-a1ac-e095d0d6a5ec" :answer/id #uuid "3ac03210-e5fe-5e87-a1ac-e095d0d6a5ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{11}+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f1a2af3-553e-59e3-9e85-b6861220aee1" :answer/id #uuid "9f1a2af3-553e-59e3-9e85-b6861220aee1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/id #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341486" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/id #uuid "4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" :question/id #uuid "c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-341500" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" :question/id #uuid "83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95616" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{1}{4})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{1}{4}}{1+\\frac{1}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}}{\\frac{5}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-67fc1eae-c999-56ca-96a1-f0623bb0224c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-67fc1eae-c999-56ca-96a1-f0623bb0224c" :answer-field/id #uuid "67fc1eae-c999-56ca-96a1-f0623bb0224c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5762675e-b9b2-5628-ae14-b2f8bf8c8a64" "answer-ef341ed2-5b5f-5140-8092-d50bef9018a5" "answer-2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" "answer-68bef3d6-95b9-53bc-bfd3-a4201accc046" "answer-45918914-cbad-5fa0-8b0f-566e642a966c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5762675e-b9b2-5628-ae14-b2f8bf8c8a64" :answer/id #uuid "5762675e-b9b2-5628-ae14-b2f8bf8c8a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef341ed2-5b5f-5140-8092-d50bef9018a5" :answer/id #uuid "ef341ed2-5b5f-5140-8092-d50bef9018a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/id #uuid "2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68bef3d6-95b9-53bc-bfd3-a4201accc046" :answer/id #uuid "68bef3d6-95b9-53bc-bfd3-a4201accc046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45918914-cbad-5fa0-8b0f-566e642a966c" :answer/id #uuid "45918914-cbad-5fa0-8b0f-566e642a966c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" :question/id #uuid "b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341494" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" :question/id #uuid "670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" :question/math-academy-id "q-126259" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\in (-2,2),$ which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{y}{2})}{1-(-\\frac{y}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50" :answer-field/id #uuid "021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" "answer-0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" "answer-570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" "answer-ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" "answer-dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" :answer/id #uuid "ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+y}{2-y})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" :answer/id #uuid "570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" :answer/id #uuid "ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y+2}{y-2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad" :answer/id #uuid "dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y-2}{y+2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" :question/id #uuid "c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125981" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles." :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" "answer-6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" "answer-2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" "answer-cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" "answer-d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" :answer/id #uuid "6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" :answer/id #uuid "2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" :answer/id #uuid "cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e" :answer/id #uuid "d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2480f399-e4b8-57fb-80b2-6dd5de7e6195" :question/id #uuid "2480f399-e4b8-57fb-80b2-6dd5de7e6195" :question/math-academy-id "q-314687" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3)$ and $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3).$ Since $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2},$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{4y-6}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-6}-2x{e}^{2y-3}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{2y-3})^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since $2y-3\\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fed67e1-a4bc-5b68-9708-4d7e1247bf7b" "answer-field-65101c58-1cdd-50ae-9a7e-f38ddece8ca6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fed67e1-a4bc-5b68-9708-4d7e1247bf7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fed67e1-a4bc-5b68-9708-4d7e1247bf7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c" :answer/id #uuid "3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65101c58-1cdd-50ae-9a7e-f38ddece8ca6" :answer-field/id #uuid "65101c58-1cdd-50ae-9a7e-f38ddece8ca6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55" :answer/id #uuid "4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" :question/id #uuid "21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341520" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ for $x\\ge 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=($\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea4e9517-341b-550d-a4d1-9501b5d7afb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea4e9517-341b-550d-a4d1-9501b5d7afb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4e9517-341b-550d-a4d1-9501b5d7afb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer/id #uuid "b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dec2af6f-6d06-5905-b90e-1ab8bd17313c" :question/id #uuid "dec2af6f-6d06-5905-b90e-1ab8bd17313c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343418" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain, and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{2(2y-3)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(2y-3)}-2x{e}^{2y-3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{2y-3})}^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we require real $y,$ we must have ${e}^{2y-3}>0.$ However, $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}<0$ for all real $x.$ Therefore, we select the positive solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-876e4b83-6790-5fe9-9bab-994d9c16699a" "answer-field-19ea8a49-836d-5084-95fa-e50271405e3e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-876e4b83-6790-5fe9-9bab-994d9c16699a" :answer-field/id #uuid "876e4b83-6790-5fe9-9bab-994d9c16699a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de" :answer/id #uuid "87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19ea8a49-836d-5084-95fa-e50271405e3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "19ea8a49-836d-5084-95fa-e50271405e3e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764" :answer/id #uuid "e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f32f1c5-eb40-50cf-b4aa-8b349651b9ca" :question/id #uuid "4f32f1c5-eb40-50cf-b4aa-8b349651b9ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-341584" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2})$ and $x\\ge 2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2}).$ Since $x\\ge 2,$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((y-2)^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 2.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}-2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})^{2}-2x({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want $y\\ge 2,$ select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}+2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e116284-e68b-5144-aec0-ae224199df97"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e116284-e68b-5144-aec0-ae224199df97" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e116284-e68b-5144-aec0-ae224199df97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938" :answer/id #uuid "d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\right)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093454 :tutorial/content "The hyperbolic functions have inverse functions that are easy to define. The **inverse hyperbolic functions** are the following:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic sine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\sinh^{-1}{x} = \\arsinh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f07f38b11cf297e099341df2ab062d4691c4502098c060de565b70f1ea7e360.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic cosine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given below.\n\n![](images/60/60607a764c50807dcb0f4f05328d900c626079654995cffbaa84fa2214788835.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic tangent**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\tanh^{-1}{x} = \\artanh{x} = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right), \\qquad x \\in (-1, 1).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given below.\n\n![](images/96/96b4f7aa06ee165751dcaa2101e9dea104d95d5bebdaea7bc4f317d9160b5a7a.png)\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\arcosh(\\sqrt2)$ we substitute $x$ for $\\sqrt2$ in the formula for $\\arcosh$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093455 :tutorial/content "We can derive the formulas from inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine, and tangent from the definition of these base functions. \n\nFor example, let's show that\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12 \\left( e^x + e^{-x} \\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, let $y = \\cosh{x}$ and $x \\geq 0.$ Then we have $y\\geq 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, now we have that $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 0.$\n\nNext, we use the definition of $\\cosh x$ and bring everything to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{y}+{e}^{-y} \\\\ 2x{e}^{y} & ={e}^{2y}+1 \\\\ {e}^{2y}-2x{e}^{y}+1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{y})}^{2}-2x({e}^{y})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $Y = e^{y},$ we form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\leq 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\geq 1.$ Since $y \\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain our formula for $\\arcosh{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x)=\n\\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092452 :activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186061044 :answer-field/choices 17592186061045] [:db/retract 17592186061044 :answer-field/choices 17592186061046] [:db/retract 17592186061044 :answer-field/choices 17592186061047] [:db/retract 17592186061044 :answer-field/choices 17592186061048] [:db/retract 17592186061044 :answer-field/choices 17592186061049] [:db/retract 17592186062269 :answer-field/choices 17592186062272] [:db/retract 17592186062269 :answer-field/choices 17592186062273] [:db/retract 17592186062269 :answer-field/choices 17592186062274] [:db/retract 17592186062269 :answer-field/choices 17592186062270] [:db/retract 17592186062269 :answer-field/choices 17592186062271] [:db/retract 17592186062071 :answer-field/choices 17592186062072] [:db/retract 17592186062071 :answer-field/choices 17592186062073] [:db/retract 17592186062071 :answer-field/choices 17592186062074] [:db/retract 17592186062071 :answer-field/choices 17592186062075] [:db/retract 17592186062071 :answer-field/choices 17592186062076] {:db/id 17592186064173 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9960d442-4263-5f59-861d-9bea9c4351f8" "question-1474faf6-6108-502e-8fd8-d08dd6e04ddc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064174 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae04db08-eab8-56dc-9a49-71b8878683c2" "question-17502c35-e040-5657-8233-e531c12d0255"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063974 :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty).$ Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We can start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty, 3]$ and $(0, \\infty)$ separately:\n\n![](images/25/255da904e7d271006cef6a272379b8f160fc6cc9e5f8ede39d91141aea2eff7b.png)\n\n![](images/31/31ad28b45aa2cefa4d83cc06710e9ae81446809ec0ac42da5baae36e235e35a9.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/6a/6a92713b9964c9b1583622553a7cdb3c9c700ecad95cc0581f1538d18d1fa42a.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty, 3]\\cap(0, \\infty)={\\color{blue}(0,3]}.$ This corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 < x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval. On the other hand, the point $3$ is included in the intersection because it lies in both intervals." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064175 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-08faca23-bc00-55b8-ac21-ab67054edd09"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063976 :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063977 :question/problem "Given the inequalities $x < 1,$  $x \\geq  0,$ and $-2 < x < 4,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval." :question/worked-solution "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9960d442-4263-5f59-861d-9bea9c4351f8" :question/id #uuid "9960d442-4263-5f59-861d-9bea9c4351f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101831" :question/problem "![](images/be/becff2a10906d3b119081b0ccd41093cdbbff7fee90c4ed440032bc46790bad1.png)\n\nWhat is the intersection of the intervals shown above, expressed  as a number line?" :question/worked-solution "The intersection of two intervals consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/94/9428f89e1751a7e91f5a869e1549af8525c9989a8c743a1acfff00430c879bae.png)\n\nTherefore, the number line that shows the common set of values is:\n\n![](images/66/66cdd62f55cb053e3e0f61a45c11b44ab0e53aa2a5732dc66772cb32471c9151.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d584ed97-b993-5dd4-b725-3d7a3f4ffff2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d584ed97-b993-5dd4-b725-3d7a3f4ffff2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d584ed97-b993-5dd4-b725-3d7a3f4ffff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a47c27ff-b988-576f-86d6-9628c6a5eb9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b92a4db-bda7-5a58-8239-0115074f3cee" "answer-464642a6-73d9-53ff-bfb3-5f251c2d5b0b" "answer-e4f3ddf8-ec18-5ba6-ad50-51dc55beceba" "answer-a47c27ff-b988-576f-86d6-9628c6a5eb9f" "answer-4ad8ea7b-1cf2-5439-90d3-243dae0ad935"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b92a4db-bda7-5a58-8239-0115074f3cee" :answer/id #uuid "4b92a4db-bda7-5a58-8239-0115074f3cee" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/17/17d75bd72023fd159d76adc4ec9618144c3a596ed5ae245e4e391d1ead36a80f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-464642a6-73d9-53ff-bfb3-5f251c2d5b0b" :answer/id #uuid "464642a6-73d9-53ff-bfb3-5f251c2d5b0b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/20/202e1967e40a092a2c74791030a9ebb738fbf85c1732f