"We start by sketching the two intervals $(-1,2)$ and $[0,3)$ separately:\n\n![](images/a5/a5a6b966c5d98863cfed20ccb13f3a1ca5413887f228f091d504bb7cd0001b37.png)\n\n![](images/82/823bf1d62aadc91ac15e52260a9919622358144865f0522f34389d48f8c11273.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/9c/9c7ea77f9699601b97ff38a886af6418b44fa9568009aaa4b36be17efa03cbee.png)\n\nThus, $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)=[0,2).$ This corresponds to the inequality $0\\le x<2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eab51b64-d5ad-523e-aa8e-5100a33e1f3f" "answer-field-f8eb0997-7805-5dd6-a59a-d474cc7a0565" "answer-field-f8be00fb-1abe-5071-b61a-57d715646d2a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eab51b64-d5ad-523e-aa8e-5100a33e1f3f" :answer-field/id #uuid "eab51b64-d5ad-523e-aa8e-5100a33e1f3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783" :answer-field/choices ["answer-413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783" :answer/id #uuid "413faa78-81b1-5d52-916b-5d7be8d5d783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8eb0997-7805-5dd6-a59a-d474cc7a0565" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8eb0997-7805-5dd6-a59a-d474cc7a0565" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f" :answer/id #uuid "05b97c4e-3c65-5ffa-ae47-8575407ba23f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8be00fb-1abe-5071-b61a-57d715646d2a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8be00fb-1abe-5071-b61a-57d715646d2a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab" :answer/id #uuid "c89bc065-1f3b-5b59-8528-9a648b77c1ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17502c35-e040-5657-8233-e531c12d0255" :question/id #uuid "17502c35-e040-5657-8233-e531c12d0255" :question/math-academy-id "q-101840" :question/problem "Find the intersection of intervals $[1,8)\\cap (4,8].$ Express the result as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "We start by sketching the two intervals $[1,8)$ and $(4,8]$ separately:\n\n![](images/63/63a236a899eff365f7964a182690470011cbee2f393586fb3a673e5332b7346e.png)\n\n![](images/9b/9b2db1951a30f71c8635e0daec81ae6931e28c2c44489952c65e10d4c95ea549.png)\n\nThe intersection $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f5/f59e4645230b48b89fb445fd3cb586516361848418e2d287e6d208c97d9dcc00.png)\n\nThus, $[1,8)\\cap (4,8]=(4,8).$ This corresponds to the inequality $4<x<8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-141c353f-f862-5687-8396-3900d4d223ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-141c353f-f862-5687-8396-3900d4d223ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "141c353f-f862-5687-8396-3900d4d223ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dadf839a-b742-583a-9087-bffd99709c40" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a17bc6c6-bbd0-5690-826b-8ef886e34033" "answer-9e97b3da-ea49-5728-95d7-76098f4e03a7" "answer-dadf839a-b742-583a-9087-bffd99709c40" "answer-db36fe97-68ba-5c44-9ec2-fa9e16dc383c