swer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a34e5c5f-3c49-58e4-83e6-1303c1acda7b" :answer/id #uuid "a34e5c5f-3c49-58e4-83e6-1303c1acda7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{145}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39fd7c9e-0ea0-51fd-96cb-49ea653f5205" :answer/id #uuid "39fd7c9e-0ea0-51fd-96cb-49ea653f5205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{147}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6868e3ac-2802-51a4-9858-c14971faab65" :answer/id #uuid "6868e3ac-2802-51a4-9858-c14971faab65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae5901e4-7a89-5d9b-9062-e68459783c38" :answer/id #uuid "ae5901e4-7a89-5d9b-9062-e68459783c38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{153}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ca05c0c-36e6-59da-9e50-1878055139fe" :answer/id #uuid "5ca05c0c-36e6-59da-9e50-1878055139fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{155}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0fca551-3459-553b-ab09-61f3dc19f41b" :question/id #uuid "d0fca551-3459-553b-ab09-61f3dc19f41b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171881" :question/problem "![](images/07/07059a0b58535952d244533e58d513f4bf538b1e2f4237888f13cc4751dab893.png)\n\nIn the figure above, the value of $x$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+(14x+{2}^{∘})+(12x+{18}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x+{20}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 34x & ={340}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81a624d5-131f-5c71-bc59-c8a7a244375c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81a624d5-131f-5c71-bc59-c8a7a244375c" :answer-field/id #uuid "81a624d5-131f-5c71-bc59-c8a7a244375c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584" :answer/id #uuid "6afe23f2-c90b-52f4-ac6a-f4d826bab584" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a5dab59-2250-5c49-b3b0-a8075568ace9" :question/id #uuid "7a5dab59-2250-5c49-b3b0-a8075568ace9" :question/math-academy-id "q-77566" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${119}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $C$ is ${103}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/6f/6fc411e7715044257a6818fe63fb3b7bd212c22a2f3d279f282b2b7161be3ecc.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $B$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{119}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠B={42}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28a9e750-d735-5da4-935f-b98288512909"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28a9e750-d735-5da4-935f-b98288512909" :answer-field/id #uuid "28a9e750-d735-5da4-935f-b98288512909" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0964060-909a-5441-84da-b4e0e78eafc5" "answer-8ca2d62e-04c1-575d-8c73-e4320dfe57e9" "answer-788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" "answer-9b6c745e-2beb-5cc5-9aca-92fb40b3e598" "answer-c9fe4011-c9fd-5df4-a4d5-65f94cbda238"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0964060-909a-5441-84da-b4e0e78eafc5" :answer/id #uuid "e0964060-909a-5441-84da-b4e0e78eafc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ca2d62e-04c1-575d-8c73-e4320dfe57e9" :answer/id #uuid "8ca2d62e-04c1-575d-8c73-e4320dfe57e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" :answer/id #uuid "788dc8c4-399c-5d30-9a77-ac4615aee599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b6c745e-2beb-5cc5-9aca-92fb40b3e598" :answer/id #uuid "9b6c745e-2beb-5cc5-9aca-92fb40b3e598" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9fe4011-c9fd-5df4-a4d5-65f94cbda238" :answer/id #uuid "c9fe4011-c9fd-5df4-a4d5-65f94cbda238" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0d1d413-28f9-5b6c-8380-80f5141c57c0" :question/id #uuid "f0d1d413-28f9-5b6c-8380-80f5141c57c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-53049" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${100}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $B$ is ${122}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](images/b1/b1d04d90855560e36762d492f34ac14fb960c98f55c234da7c706e638fd9ea9e.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{100}^{∘}+{122}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠C={42}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec105581-d8ab-5cfb-9904-990edbb43675"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec105581-d8ab-5cfb-9904-990edbb43675" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec105581-d8ab-5cfb-9904-990edbb43675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8af4d36e-fe88-5a74-8ad7-fe65d8abc1ca" "answer-e058fdbb-c7c1-5343-b0b2-46fb3b24d7b0" "answer-aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" "answer-af344a1a-674e-54cf-8766-1a8770d632bc" "answer-60d2dd30-298a-557e-bc40-f2d7d3620958"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8af4d36e-fe88-5a74-8ad7-fe65d8abc1ca" :answer/id #uuid "8af4d36e-fe88-5a74-8ad7-fe65d8abc1ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e058fdbb-c7c1-5343-b0b2-46fb3b24d7b0" :answer/id #uuid "e058fdbb-c7c1-5343-b0b2-46fb3b24d7b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" :answer/id #uuid "aacbffa7-8c20-5000-9115-ca5b2b62f092" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af344a1a-674e-54cf-8766-1a8770d632bc" :answer/id #uuid "af344a1a-674e-54cf-8766-1a8770d632bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60d2dd30-298a-557e-bc40-f2d7d3620958" :answer/id #uuid "60d2dd30-298a-557e-bc40-f2d7d3620958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{46}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111843 :tutorial/content "Consider the following triangle, where the side ![](images/72/721aa9559f1b9f3da3e69b1b9763327e29a6f3fb77157f4b42dd076d3865e098.svg) has been extended beyond the vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\n  \n\nThe resulting angle ![](images/f1/f1729f131b4ec04b45c992a99498753dd09d27e76e917dff66d1fd554ec2ab5e.svg) is called the **exterior angle** at vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) \n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n![](images/9a/9ae2a4aa323b941eff72a50f5c1086c72e961d0eed919a327306e77cfd5a7ef6.svg)\n\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to ![](images/1a/1ace39b6f52fbf0b8654fc88bfa3e97366d33e3c411580da106e513a498aca53.svg) we have \n![](images/49/49aafbdb975ccd101e16c921c20630af6296b860438ebfb62d8a205cc6e7b3c7.svg)\n\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\n![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111844 :tutorial/content "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\nThe sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have \n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =m∠B+m∠C \\\\ y & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ z & =m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =(m∠B+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠B) \\\\  & =2m∠A+2m∠B+2m∠C \\\\  & =2(m∠A+m∠B+m∠C) \\\\  & =2({180}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110830 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186086244 :answer-field/choices 17592186086245] [:db/retract 17592186086244 :answer-field/choices 17592186086246] [:db/retract 17592186086244 :answer-field/choices 17592186086247] [:db/retract 17592186086244 :answer-field/choices 17592186086248] [:db/retract 17592186086244 :answer-field/choices 17592186086249] [:db/retract 17592186086250 :answer-field/choices 17592186086251] [:db/retract 17592186086250 :answer-field/choices 17592186086252] [:db/retract 17592186086250 :answer-field/choices 17592186086253] [:db/retract 17592186086250 :answer-field/choices 17592186086254] [:db/retract 17592186086250 :answer-field/choices 17592186086255] {:db/id 17592186087816 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-326c594b-0df4-5454-9bfb-36d7406d2334" "question-3149fa14-6168-5e70-a022-3a17c27c06aa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087424 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)$ defined by the following table? \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The domain is the set of all input values for which the function is defined. In the table, this corresponds to the set of $x$-values in the first row:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\{ -2,-1,0,2,4,6\\}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087423 :question/worked-solution "The function above is defined for values between $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-2,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=2,$ this point is also included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write this as\n\n$$\nx \\in [-2,2].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087817 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5a26c2b4-7f63-57f6-895a-eaed89dc8c51" "question-f88054ee-2e9d-5199-bfa3-4c51d0b9da47"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087652 :question/worked-solution "The function above is defined for all values of $x$ that are less than $x = 3.$\n\nIn addition, since there is a closed circle at $x = 3,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is $x \\leq 3,$ which we can write as $x \\in (-\\infty, 3].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-326c594b-0df4-5454-9bfb-36d7406d2334" :question/id #uuid "326c594b-0df4-5454-9bfb-36d7406d2334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130437" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3af5c931067f960d2099efdb9a19e23dd2835a16095cdc0e61a0a507b0e8a3c.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f$ defined by the mapping diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "The domain is the set of all input values for which the function is defined. In the mapping diagram, this corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set:\n\n$$\n\\{5,10,15,20\\}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02fb490d-7974-523d-bc5c-3d147361beac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02fb490d-7974-523d-bc5c-3d147361beac" :answer-field/id #uuid "02fb490d-7974-523d-bc5c-3d147361beac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-847fff66-ddfc-5162-bb0b-bd2399256f64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ce20594-2358-5ff7-89ff-5cadd0a95e97" "answer-cc91d0ee-3d52-5f3e-938e-dd040c2caf25" "answer-f3c1b6f7-300e-54f8-945b-311c518e0e48" "answer-847fff66-ddfc-5162-bb0b-bd2399256f64" "answer-f1381d05-6389-52f2-9c1d-61eb3554de91"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ce20594-2358-5ff7-89ff-5cadd0a95e97" :answer/id #uuid "1ce20594-2358-5ff7-89ff-5cadd0a95e97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,20)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc91d0ee-3d52-5f3e-938e-dd040c2caf25" :answer/id #uuid "cc91d0ee-3d52-5f3e-938e-dd040c2caf25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[5,20]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3c1b6f7-300e-54f8-945b-311c518e0e48" :answer/id #uuid "f3c1b6f7-300e-54f8-945b-311c518e0e48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[6,24]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-847fff66-ddfc-5162-bb0b-bd2399256f64" :answer/id #uuid "847fff66-ddfc-5162-bb0b-bd2399256f64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{5,10,15,20\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1381d05-6389-52f2-9c1d-61eb3554de91" :answer/id #uuid "f1381d05-6389-52f2-9c1d-61eb3554de91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{6,12,18,24\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3149fa14-6168-5e70-a022-3a17c27c06aa" :question/id #uuid "3149fa14-6168-5e70-a022-3a17c27c06aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-51993" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5ead632d2afb4ac2c735994984ee0347334bb5f34a6302fa8399615a3f757149.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f$ defined by the mapping diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "The domain is the set of all input values for which the function is defined. In the mapping diagram, this corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set:\n\n$$\n\\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2},1\\}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afe93943-e0af-59e2-9a28-b38df5dd670a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afe93943-e0af-59e2-9a28-b38df5dd670a" :answer-field/id #uuid "afe93943-e0af-59e2-9a28-b38df5dd670a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91ef59e0-71cd-5b63-9177-e97e46334f45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aafd322c-e874-5a05-9fa5-0d463a8c60f8" "answer-1d5ee072-a186-5801-baeb-72bf9081afdb" "answer-91ef59e0-71cd-5b63-9177-e97e46334f45" "answer-975e8ac9-c6dc-57e5-8f57-39324a6766d9" "answer-210db4ff-04db-54c7-a564-a53b3c3b7d94"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aafd322c-e874-5a05-9fa5-0d463a8c60f8" :answer/id #uuid "aafd322c-e874-5a05-9fa5-0d463a8c60f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d5ee072-a186-5801-baeb-72bf9081afdb" :answer/id #uuid "1d5ee072-a186-5801-baeb-72bf9081afdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91ef59e0-71cd-5b63-9177-e97e46334f45" :answer/id #uuid "91ef59e0-71cd-5b63-9177-e97e46334f45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2},1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975e8ac9-c6dc-57e5-8f57-39324a6766d9" :answer/id #uuid "975e8ac9-c6dc-57e5-8f57-39324a6766d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{2,8,64\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-210db4ff-04db-54c7-a564-a53b3c3b7d94" :answer/id #uuid "210db4ff-04db-54c7-a564-a53b3c3b7d94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{8\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088265 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there is an open circle at $x=4,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x<4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186086244 :answer-field/correct "answer-4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1cc05a0f-f981-5af5-a7e1-d40a3d4c4527" "answer-f4b827e3-4c97-500b-86e9-6831c7386dd8" "answer-4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" "answer-97b4b334-2f21-576b-a08b-fd2f13db8840" "answer-98ec4914-a43d-55aa-9b2f-cbf99a119ed4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cc05a0f-f981-5af5-a7e1-d40a3d4c4527" :answer/id #uuid "1cc05a0f-f981-5af5-a7e1-d40a3d4c4527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (1,4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4b827e3-4c97-500b-86e9-6831c7386dd8" :answer/id #uuid "f4b827e3-4c97-500b-86e9-6831c7386dd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (1,5]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" :answer/id #uuid "4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [1,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97b4b334-2f21-576b-a08b-fd2f13db8840" :answer/id #uuid "97b4b334-2f21-576b-a08b-fd2f13db8840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [1,4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98ec4914-a43d-55aa-9b2f-cbf99a119ed4" :answer/id #uuid "98ec4914-a43d-55aa-9b2f-cbf99a119ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [1,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088266 :question/worked-solution "The function above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=4,$ this point is also included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186086250 :answer-field/correct "answer-210be75c-0a59-5e12-98cb-2b93c04ffa4a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d0ae3342-6361-57b1-8ba7-6a0afffc75ac" "answer-3002da76-1787-5945-87ca-71f107662ad0" "answer-210be75c-0a59-5e12-98cb-2b93c04ffa4a" "answer-26105266-fdf8-5e7e-84a5-e7bd07a284af" "answer-311f56c5-9585-5a8f-8e7f-73c086425ea9"] :db/ensure [:answer-fi