wer-9ce58caf-403c-5cb1-b5f9-576933072db7" "answer-17266884-c7ab-5455-bfcc-86b2e6e1f07e" "answer-f1e4e66e-4610-5a67-a08e-8d19751ea41f" "answer-f6c32a1b-f934-5b24-8c03-974acdfed50f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e340067-2445-57c4-ba59-4ed2e49b5413" :answer/id #uuid "9e340067-2445-57c4-ba59-4ed2e49b5413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ce58caf-403c-5cb1-b5f9-576933072db7" :answer/id #uuid "9ce58caf-403c-5cb1-b5f9-576933072db7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17266884-c7ab-5455-bfcc-86b2e6e1f07e" :answer/id #uuid "17266884-c7ab-5455-bfcc-86b2e6e1f07e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1e4e66e-4610-5a67-a08e-8d19751ea41f" :answer/id #uuid "f1e4e66e-4610-5a67-a08e-8d19751ea41f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6c32a1b-f934-5b24-8c03-974acdfed50f" :answer/id #uuid "f6c32a1b-f934-5b24-8c03-974acdfed50f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-2,6]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f88054ee-2e9d-5199-bfa3-4c51d0b9da47" :question/id #uuid "f88054ee-2e9d-5199-bfa3-4c51d0b9da47" :question/math-academy-id "q-130659" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0a972432a38ab5081bd60db3fbb6bc00f81c2672c81ab6fc5027cee1b88b809.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The function above is defined for all values of $x$ other than $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ea6d9b2-093f-51be-a58b-49ddd428adad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ea6d9b2-093f-51be-a58b-49ddd428adad" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea6d9b2-093f-51be-a58b-49ddd428adad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d09d52d7-0f46-5bd6-b36a-38755a9567c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-01183f01-992f-5a34-8298-4be6d5d73976" "answer-d09d52d7-0f46-5bd6-b36a-38755a9567c0" "answer-28bd0717-9921-5919-9e62-33a8eba93b72" "answer-12eda399-a8d7-53f2-a8f0-aad90ef36064" "answer-09f080a6-b427-563b-ae55-b03d337c2cd1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01183f01-992f-5a34-8298-4be6d5d73976" :answer/id #uuid "01183f01-992f-5a34-8298-4be6d5d73976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d09d52d7-0f46-5bd6-b36a-38755a9567c0" :answer/id #uuid "d09d52d7-0f46-5bd6-b36a-38755a9567c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28bd0717-9921-5919-9e62-33a8eba93b72" :answer/id #uuid "28bd0717-9921-5919-9e62-33a8eba93b72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12eda399-a8d7-53f2-a8f0-aad90ef36064" :answer/id #uuid "12eda399-a8d7-53f2-a8f0-aad90ef36064" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-5,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09f080a6-b427-563b-ae55-b03d337c2cd1" :answer/id #uuid "09f080a6-b427-563b-ae55-b03d337c2cd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in \\{-5,-4,-3,-2,0,1,2,3\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088981 :tutorial/content "The **domain** of a function is the set of all input values for which it is defined.\n\nFor example, consider the function represented by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c39c6cdd67f1b69248b38e009b6de12acef250a59543aca2d2922ec1801d57.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set. Therefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where a function is defined using a table." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088866 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a666cbc79869f13f7079db92396ae6f7ad2b2041b2aa0ea704149119a850eed.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0,$ nor is it defined for $x > 2.$\n\n- The function has filled circles at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ so it is defined at both of these points.\n\n- In addition, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nWe can also express the domain using interval notation:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nThe square brackets $[\\:]$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are included in the domain." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088982 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=g(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c99d3cbb1b7ba516cc2cf75856aeb17b9f91d23185d6fd25319a492ca4e3b164.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0,$ nor is it defined for $x > 2.$\n\n- The function has open circles at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ so it is *not* defined at either of these points.\n\n- However, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\lt 2,\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\:)$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are excluded from the domain." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088983 :tutorial/content "It's possible for a function to have one endpoint included in the domain and another endpoint excluded. \n\nFor example, consider the function is $y=h(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0919c9b416ad49ed08d566b8c85d250a1a00a3ebf72f4d22c3d608fb42ede169.png)\n\nThe endpoint $x=-2$ is included in the domain, but the endpoint $x=2$ is excluded. Therefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x \\lt 2,\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in[-2, 2).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088865 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)=2x,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2660977b21e476e1a295e215ac22888625763b12b1685521c289adf8326f78c.png)\n\nNo matter which number $x$ we pick as an input, $f(x)$ always will return an output. Therefore, we can write the domain of this function as \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the function $g(x) = \\dfrac 1 x,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/84/84fefed3d54e57467ca3ebcf823c6bbf5c3c6aacce2f7542230b1abacfd97f46.png)\n\nWe cannot substitute the value $x=0$ into the function $g(x)$ because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not part of the domain of $g(x).$ However, we can use any other number $x$ as input for $g(x).$ Therefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty, 0)\\cup (0,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis notation means that any number *apart from zero* can be used as an input for $g(x).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087891 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100929 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-836f7fef-3457-5df4-8168-19818aab4136" "question-afcdf6e0-b120-55bc-9035-712918708d21" "question-62b94dd7-cfc2-59b5-b47b-429c83f35bf4" "question-e93d51a5-9dcf-5b93-bb29-2d6994b5330e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100762 :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n![](images/1f/1fcbb5677ad7c8f630ccd7d194ef92f59fe3424d6bdb85e20f1ae522567b94ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ So, $f''(x) >  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e1749906-d3a8-5b9b-9937-44d64a8b3739" "question-beaea1c2-cac1-535c-a0ba-94e307b8fec5" "question-bee43d68-1db4-514c-9b20-dd6368dba765" "question-3c2bbcbb-181d-5929-9b37-8caf2befb3ea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100638 :question/problem "The graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/d9/d9746bb18a0e3ae662e477173e4da8b6599245edb02003287d83ab5e894aa814.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x \\approx 2.$ So $f''(2)\\approx0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(2)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x \\approx 2,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8f8c622296689efa9290c0ecb219abdba20cf71052ca77df1a131658d534a68.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63a13aa0-4488-51d5-bbbc-b4c2f1626c48" "question-15879841-b72a-52ec-bd4a-969c8835b3a6" "question-e3e7c579-7100-5dba-9db2-7857d6271cd6" "question-2b0e787a-88a1-5ceb-86de-c3a98e9d2857" "question-d1651c23-1a6f-5db8-8bb9-875d0a212ede"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100763 :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below. Sketch the graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n![](images/ed/ed26d7560d9bd7294bf35a8e0e52292a7b5922d419303c63430c73c32c63070e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get $f'(x) <         0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $\\textrm{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x \\in (-\\infty,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is an increasing straight line. So, $y=f'(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\n- For $x \\in (-1,\\infty),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, $f''(x) <  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y=f'(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d00aadf7b1666ea8a762833d1844d638a224759c58d1b06929657ddb90dc4abb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c0c91e8-a70d-5113-bcb2-8f7a22529a09" "question-e2c85a10-a5e4-538c-9cb8-85994d6879ad" "question-60da6948-77d4-526b-84a9-f5ff89c1d7b4" "question-0028aa3d-01ab-5dd0-b507-a0e026d36abf" "question-4fa7b9a4-0348-582a-97ef-22ec70d27b82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100639 :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f''(x).$\n\n![](images/bf/bf8bde9f9b5060fbf14a329833f35ce9970c850e805e0c551cccaa70d6288c06.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x) = f'(x)$ then $f''(x)=g'(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y=g'(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $g'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $g'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $g'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=g(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $g'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x \\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25.$ At the inflection points, we have $g''(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y=g'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x\\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $g'(1)$ and $g'(2.25),$ but from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that at $x \\approx 1$ and $x\\approx 2.25$ the slope is zero and negative, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=g'(x)=f''(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/78/785b221ae245b389e707f8189c91e57af888ecbb44dfce384ab1c60958d91a6f.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-836f7fef-3457-5df4-8168-19818aab4136" :question/id #uuid "836f7fef-3457-5df4-8168-19818aab4136" :question/math-academy-id "q-20255" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2b5a3500e8e3adf2023a1bfb7fabcd27398b029b87f474d00ffb7ac76b90e2a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2.5)$ | $2.5$ | $(2.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fde284c6bda2a0a9dba79a41348cf25ce7d5e5db8d2af996c577ceca721d5ab2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf7a2783-6857-5476-8287-08af426693c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf7a2783-6857-5476-8287-08af426693c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf7a2783-6857-5476-8287-08af426693c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c6f0426-db1f-5cb3-b483-6634da4706fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6727d3e0-7cad-5db4-9464-92d36323a34f" "answer-63581de7-e105-556b-8269-b74a607c6547" "answer-277a2d03-83e1-5f35-888c-224cc21dd025" "answer-a50b0c8d-cb5d-5c81-af7c-9bc59ba5c470" "answer-3c6f0426-db1f-5cb3-b483-6634da4706fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6727d3e0-7cad-5db4-9464-92d36323a34f" :answer/id #uuid "6727d3e0-7cad-5db4-9464-92d36323a34f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/24/246fdeb5ee754fae2741a42e1645d0b09dec54ec9fcb175474b8dafaa0e352bf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63581de7-e105-556b-8269-b74a607c6547" :answer/id #uuid "63581de7-e105-556b-8269-b74a607c6547" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4b/4b3d6a49625f963c9744c387e353d47948d3ce7fb2a405f258984faa6853c6e5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-277a2d03-83e1-5f35-888c-224cc21dd025" :answer/id #uuid "277a2d03-83e1-5f35-888c-224cc21dd025" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/75/75a8103219a95d991891315a5e3ae6c6ac5e938dd1a1601ccc8aafe09004997d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a50b0c8d-cb5d-5c81-af7c-9bc59ba5c470" :answer/id #uuid "a50b0c8d-cb5d-5c81-af7c-9bc59ba5c470" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/db/db70198bd47e56995f2e50a3b68788c863034574622c383670c115de1dbec08f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c6f0426-db1f-5cb3-b483-6634da4706fc" :answer/id #uuid "3c6f0426-db1f-5cb3-b483-6634da4706fc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fd/fde284c6bda2a0a9dba79a41348cf25ce7d5e5db8d2af996c577ceca721d5ab2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-afcdf6e0-b120-55bc-9035-712918708d21" :question/id #uuid "afcdf6e0-b120-55bc-9035-712918708d21" :question/math-academy-id "q-114593" :question/problem "![](images/34/347ddfa6a11cd4904f0e790a55a611edbb6ac89d4a4a2190627bd318ad2af7dd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0.5)$ | $0.5$ | $(0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22f1c9da3a225f641aadfc6e244e54fbf49a22e58724b937c73502181f91681c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3ad2a19-9ee2-55dd-b140-b09c354ee276"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3ad2a19-9ee2-55dd-b140-b09c354ee276" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ad2a19-9ee2-55dd-b140-b09c354ee276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" "answer-c2e5e598-abff-5bb2-aeb0-e10923fb9cf5" "answer-78e1e403-872f-5281-a201-3491e337f8a2" "answer-62714582-555d-5e26-a461-854ac6245d1f" "answer-424d0541-3986-5328-a50f-2382f1d98417"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" :answer/id #uuid "bd1c47a9-5056-518a-95ee-d284e33e8200" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/22/22f1c9da3a225f641aadfc6e244e54fbf49a22e58724b937c73502181f91681c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2e5e598-abff-5bb2-aeb0-e10923fb9cf5" :answer/id #uuid "c2e5e598-abff-5bb2-aeb0-e10923fb9cf5" :