rom the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8cd5fc7e-847e-5ca9-aca9-1c3905f8545b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cd5fc7e-847e-5ca9-aca9-1c3905f8545b" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd5fc7e-847e-5ca9-aca9-1c3905f8545b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6478e87-3a1c-58dd-9745-b0944fd5ebba" "answer-065c16e1-4085-50a6-bb78-007ef4782272" "answer-e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" "answer-e12dd70e-42cb-57e7-94b7-a9810647facf" "answer-fe7487c3-4a91-5600-8a62-a7fa78decdbb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6478e87-3a1c-58dd-9745-b0944fd5ebba" :answer/id #uuid "b6478e87-3a1c-58dd-9745-b0944fd5ebba" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0c/0cd84966048643abfd83c950d49ec7d9318c177cfdf062f58c502f7d966c7c29.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-065c16e1-4085-50a6-bb78-007ef4782272" :answer/id #uuid "065c16e1-4085-50a6-bb78-007ef4782272" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/50/5045b953f12139167ac6498f8d379b0b89379344a1e3f04f555d35c39e87248a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" :answer/id #uuid "e71f34a5-a7ea-5111-80bb-b97aaedf2b7e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/57/57e2244806cb3b1168da1a1be30f923b7ee721431addd926654fcb4e51c2063b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12dd70e-42cb-57e7-94b7-a9810647facf" :answer/id #uuid "e12dd70e-42cb-57e7-94b7-a9810647facf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a1/a107d619837beb87b1690ac8cc6ea500f374de3d5d4138d5a6a29ff32daf6ac9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe7487c3-4a91-5600-8a62-a7fa78decdbb" :answer/id #uuid "fe7487c3-4a91-5600-8a62-a7fa78decdbb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d4/d41ffdc4ea9c6bb9b3dfb66924aabe95a497f26e0cca6b97a3a533ea8a864bd3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101301 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a44ff10b8b99077e742024dc360563e2481871a44592f1fd9260947dba82dfed.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/affedc1cf175ac4fb632345fcf45d5facbc29ecbe6ce5f4a2c31800a22e2e170.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099127 :answer-field/correct 17592186099132 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-beaea1c2-cac1-535c-a0ba-94e307b8fec5" :question/id #uuid "beaea1c2-cac1-535c-a0ba-94e307b8fec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16216" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1ae30d99b72eaa6267e28951f87b77d0ed02b1a64d59efce03a9708090b00ca3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf15827c-2aaf-5f53-bd58-4814c2cd9492"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf15827c-2aaf-5f53-bd58-4814c2cd9492" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf15827c-2aaf-5f53-bd58-4814c2cd9492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc080b55-562f-5acc-913f-1c61a64683d9" "answer-2a4a53ea-9228-5174-a635-63c1d66e653b" "answer-127803c3-67f4-5702-8fea-c46c384133c9" "answer-44244673-86b2-5305-94b0-6980c7258aa0" "answer-d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc080b55-562f-5acc-913f-1c61a64683d9" :answer/id #uuid "dc080b55-562f-5acc-913f-1c61a64683d9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3f/3f43012be791a13962a3b41cab736c04f2ad9fe7ad965af5e8ea23bf1dfa07cb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a4a53ea-9228-5174-a635-63c1d66e653b" :answer/id #uuid "2a4a53ea-9228-5174-a635-63c1d66e653b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5e/5ec0265dbebeeff2efb1ccb8d8e1d2d74a213fad091872b4366445a66a36bacc.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-127803c3-67f4-5702-8fea-c46c384133c9" :answer/id #uuid "127803c3-67f4-5702-8fea-c46c384133c9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6f/6f76708a1a70a14e10ce6b041186f4610f4a4b4c5b32ba4ac4dc3d310f6fdd17.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44244673-86b2-5305-94b0-6980c7258aa0" :answer/id #uuid "44244673-86b2-5305-94b0-6980c7258aa0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b5/b584eceb1fc1189e1a17b936222a3acb48987d319dab42a70c24aebb99df3a64.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe" :answer/id #uuid "d5c327c0-d3e9-5c13-b2a4-68c7efadd0fe" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d0/d0c1765a4b35cbbfc8fdb4ee6182937d7abcd53aa779d63066f6b7937128bec3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bee43d68-1db4-514c-9b20-dd6368dba765" :question/id #uuid "bee43d68-1db4-514c-9b20-dd6368dba765" :question/math-academy-id "q-20238" :question/problem "![](images/63/6364cdaa6ace7feb4b8ed53dc30738f019e97a915e2d46aca5c71f4b33fff1bf.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1.4)$ | $1.4$ | $(1.4,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.75.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.75)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.75)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.75,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b86a0a6f67463532a765c3ce85cb58da229ffa4e83ffd755610371e6c089b2b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f4f5425-2516-5054-8553-6855f1c18d2d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f4f5425-2516-5054-8553-6855f1c18d2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4f5425-2516-5054-8553-6855f1c18d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8430c48e-2188-5143-878f-214905f90d34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7a39322-d78c-5b92-9813-d64c89655e10" "answer-8430c48e-2188-5143-878f-214905f90d34" "answer-e40399cc-bbdb-5f72-924c-c9b32bbede3e" "answer-efcae7e2-c062-5950-b721-7fc049630ca1" "answer-f978c937-d8e6-58e3-8836-cd731d7bc6c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a39322-d78c-5b92-9813-d64c89655e10" :answer/id #uuid "f7a39322-d78c-5b92-9813-d6