nary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2c5472a0-c79b-54dc-863f-15b3152ef90d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2c5472a0-c79b-54dc-863f-15b3152ef90d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c5472a0-c79b-54dc-863f-15b3152ef90d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ea84576-2e0d-5ee2-8f21-06257827a47b" "answer-58a4ac8a-9ad5-59f6-8074-8fc6edc9b565" "answer-e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" "answer-db0be5f6-e260-5fed-988b-f8affb29f9d3" "answer-94287a7b-ef3c-5924-8031-1edcb4ad269b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ea84576-2e0d-5ee2-8f21-06257827a47b" :answer/id #uuid "7ea84576-2e0d-5ee2-8f21-06257827a47b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0e/0e73b329105ffe0c8fa1b4f4e9c18a2015d135b37019074de350dd7a57c10d5c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58a4ac8a-9ad5-59f6-8074-8fc6edc9b565" :answer/id #uuid "58a4ac8a-9ad5-59f6-8074-8fc6edc9b565" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/26/26aa00905a5decb29f8f5a71825b225390392333d4be375e8f5018fea7c44fc9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" :answer/id #uuid "e5cc33d7-500a-5599-9848-8394db27e68f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6b/6b738f1d76c14e03e048ca751dbfa416f05e415ca3cf1729fef6821d7d44d00f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db0be5f6-e260-5fed-988b-f8affb29f9d3" :answer/id #uuid "db0be5f6-e260-5fed-988b-f8affb29f9d3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bd/bd4a3c1360880a5c39f2f0366bbfeae264998515845c7dfb097d9803aa4a3ccc.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94287a7b-ef3c-5924-8031-1edcb4ad269b" :answer/id #uuid "94287a7b-ef3c-5924-8031-1edcb4ad269b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/cc/cc425d19b4f25ddea6e52dd5267ce895aa1598dc19085eef0f9d832188f77f71.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63a13aa0-4488-51d5-bbbc-b4c2f1626c48" :question/id #uuid "63a13aa0-4488-51d5-bbbc-b4c2f1626c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-114646" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1daad2a8baabf38dabf1dfe5f8d7e0edf4f3ac8645145f2d0513fc2c59fc26e0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\text{const.}$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $0$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from  $0$ to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/7b/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d848507f-dcf4-5e8d-8a83-e80e1621fe7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d848507f-dcf4-5e8d-8a83-e80e1621fe7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "d848507f-dcf4-5e8d-8a83-e80e1621fe7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31557e3b-d4b0-5267-aa9a-7e150496c677" "answer-fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" "answer-a044db3e-be10-5ff0-bb44-4b2a8ffcbe05" "answer-3e94616f-6a31-55bf-b92a-1fa270b2ee0f" "answer-13b33ec3-5950-5c5d-bc79-771c3f1f6633"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31557e3b-d4b0-5267-aa9a-7e150496c677" :answer/id #uuid "31557e3b-d4b0-5267-aa9a-7e150496c677" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/46/466a429d3a5bac24bf0f8bc8a67750fec14d7b7ce211fc0c6bc429881fa7c923.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" :answer/id #uuid "fbb2bdd5-9d4f-5154-85fe-9b53c449bdda" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7b/7bef73fc655d0953a315bdd6f9524d0527e54c65453f3359059df7d43c444de7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a044db3e-be10-5ff0-bb44-4b2a8ffcbe05" :answer/id #uuid "a044db3e-be10-5ff0-bb44-4b2a8ffcbe05" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b4/b40a9c3fb1a90f5e25a7bd2d95fca4e36c378317f1b41c48ae7cd53a1d2c5e3d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e94616f-6a31-55bf-b92a-1fa270b2ee0f" :answer/id #uuid "3e94616f-6a31-55bf-b92a-1fa270b2ee0f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/df/df71cb03d76b04cb315575fc91fe0c0f55635abe790684773e56d780ab66a0a4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13b33ec3-5950-5c5d-bc79-771c3f1f6633" :answer/id #uuid "13b33ec3-5950-5c5d-bc79-771c3f1f6633" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f6/f68914b468166f15e7529357c336fec2d47337b6dac58ae105ee7f368d2b78d1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-15879841-b72a-52ec-bd4a-969c8835b3a6" :question/id #uuid "15879841-b72a-52ec-bd4a-969c8835b3a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114648" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1de00f79b0621d8b9f5f5731f4796bd53d3319451deca953bc12cfb4c24f65d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to positive) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62a3a174-4905-5d32-9ded-ef2a2049d3eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62a3a174-4905-5d32-9ded-ef2a2049d3eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "62a3a174-4905-5d32-9ded-ef2a2049d3eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef050412-4c8f-52e9-b6a2-3a607cf09959" "answer-a533fdf3-5be1-5597-b740-16199c88b5bd" "answer-109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" "answer-6580ea04-9e4d-5252-a7be-8ca4d068a89b" "answer-a7c32281-4d07-5460-a1b2-3495c04c942a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef050412-4c8f-52e9-b6a2-3a607cf09959" :answer/id #uuid "ef050412-4c8f-52e9-b6a2-3a607cf09959" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/01/01f0a91835e9337feac6340286c599b5ef29ac307bc29acc9a4a159c3d58d4f5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a533fdf3-5be1-5597-b740-16199c88b5bd" :answer/id #uuid "a533fdf3-5be1-5597-b740-16199c88b5bd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/04/048a95c8960c30ef089b3d69032a5423e7ae11663bd1eb7ff97815298b667e9a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" :answer/id #uuid "109603d2-dcfe-5abe-b525-db503e1c609e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/17/17ccf1c0863b25fd8cb0d4227c43c071e3e24f35831ae1eed6c262aca353ba08.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6580ea04-9e4d-5252-a7be-8ca4d068a89b" :answer/id #uuid "6580ea04-9e4d-5252-a7be-8ca4d068a89b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/90/9017bf057696001853904341f5479243a29ffe4faa435039499f32e7f90ad594.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7c32281-4d07-5460-a1b2-3495c04c942a" :answer/id #uuid "a7c32281-4d07-5460-a1b2-3495c04c942a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/99/9973f597f4d21f5e17b58939279aa1b4c5e25d890bcea5aad32129202d571f26.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3e7c579-7100-5dba-9db2-7857d6271cd6" :question/id #uuid "e3e7c579-7100-5dba-9db2-7857d6271cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51419" :question/problem "![](images/47/472b1c50cef2f5ce7be11fc83eda45775e734125ba0572305ab828dc7ed278b4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is a increasing straight line. So, $y={f}^{′}(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf8e039b-69a8-5499-971f-81bd40925631"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf8e039b-69a8-5499-971f-81bd40925631" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8e039b-69a8-5499-971f-81bd40925631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-438cd5cc-aedc-50cc-ac57-0cc4ccfc5949" "answer-914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" "answer-544e3cde-3241-57d1-8784-3d33f7e52e6b" "answer-9aefdebf-0e8a-59bd-bf33-5a57915efa2e" "answer-99c4a7c9-b8b6-5d1a-8631-bb06f3c8f9a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-438cd5cc-aedc-50cc-ac57-0cc4ccfc5949" :answer/id #uuid "438cd5cc-aedc-50cc-ac57-0cc4ccfc5949" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/01/017d8876b1dda0c8c9d94e28b93c12f756cd030d044a638a15e73a80da64f5b0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" :answer/id #uuid "914c8377-0df2-5fd6-876a-b3998921af10" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1c/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-544e3cde-3241-57d1-8784-3d33f7e52e6b" :answer/id #uuid "544e3cde-3241-57d1-8784-3d33f7e52e6b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/20/2060cb11e8f0402d74ef1baa3a287c7e9cef9ca58e6efa98923538cb53128f27.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9aefdebf-0e8a-59bd-bf33-5a57915efa2e" :answer/id #uuid "9aefdebf-0e8a-59bd-bf33-5a57915efa2e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/29/29e46d9b0874bc7c16031ae122890a0b4091db3c92174701b8ca1e5dae7f2917.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99c4a7c9-b8b6-5d1a-8631-bb06f3c8f9a4" :answer/id #uuid "99c4a7c9-b8b6-5d1a-8631-bb06f3c8f9a4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/31/31edaa2afe89ceccd2fe434d428ce333b909b0a46c6eeb14d216d254c062e8ff.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b0e787a-88a1-5ceb-86de-c3a98e9d2857" :question/id #uuid "2b0e787a-88a1-5ceb-86de-c3a98e9d2857" :question/math-academy-id "q-114643" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d69caa35281c084636fbbf530b40c264ad4cdd7e626f941be67d3c0e0405a4ba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to  $0$) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0fd00428-35f0-519d-8d68-450f9941cf18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0fd00428-35f0-519d-8d68-450f9941cf18" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd00428-35f0-519d-8d68-450f9941cf18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71818e85-b7aa-5b18-873a-26dbd767a46b" "answer-a559e948-fc3e-5a00-97da-c0675be541c9" "answer-ca22e61b-de36-50ae-8128-7bf2c8de7e64" "answer-90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" "answer-3defae3c-884f-5ea8-80de-1e621559316b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71818e85-b7aa-5b18-873a-26dbd767a46b" :answer/id #uuid "71818e85-b7aa-5b18-873a-26dbd767a46b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4a/4aa24ed382e7591e79ae293316c2ce1b976a3ea4cf87a602e7577e5de2d44e14.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a559e948-fc3e-5a00-97da-c0675be541c9" :answer/id #uuid "a559e948-fc3e-5a00-97da-c0675be541c9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/64/64e6bc97e55e414cfa9bfe2003cc6bcf0be546e657bf8397535974d4b1861d74.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca22e61b-de36-50ae-8128-7bf2c8de7e64" :answer/id #uuid "ca22e61b-de36-50ae-8128-7bf2c8de7e64" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/68/68e94fb9500dc51ed5dbae1d1987ff8dc2450d3cc8ad91e3161d3d367631f173.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" :answer/id #uuid "90fddf5f-5fed-55db-ae50-af4e3de480a8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/88/88f858ca236aabbf86103dbd6a5227821e7b2bf9611dde0872a6a1e34bc6b931.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3defae3c-884f-5ea8-80de-1e621559316b" :answer/id #uuid "3defae3c-884f-5ea8-80de-1e621559316b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ff/ffb86bfbcedbb021c61571aefce757752ff5d395629a8717e348bd173cd06b30.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d1651c23-1a6f-5db8-8bb9-875d0a212ede" :question/id #uuid "d1651c23-1a6f-5db8-8bb9-875d0a212ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-51265" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fe60368dc9dddb25380d76a34e8d3d7d2b2db4aa61e1d3333dda8242c37ccb0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/96/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c31977d0-78ff-535d-bc38-407ef7d45814"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c31977d0-78ff-535d-bc38-407ef7d45814" :answer-field/id #uuid "c31977d0-78ff-535d-bc38-407ef7d45814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2650ef17-0fcb-51fd-a9eb-eff25cdbe18b" "answer-52c1b36d-6dec-5472-8e55-0c2af00b581a" "answer-b7564118-cc79-52d5-895d-26764e2194cb" "answer-ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" "answer-8f56a479-bb17-5e09-be93-fcf9d34e974c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2650ef17-0fcb-51fd-a9eb-eff25cdbe18b" :answer/id #uuid "2650ef17-0fcb-51fd-a9eb-eff25cdbe18b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1e/1e83096f6dacd597fcab59f6dd7ae52f9a52133c59a5c6fb3f1bf0250b42032d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c1b36d-6dec-5472-8e55-0c2af00b581a" :answer/id #uuid "52c1b36d-6dec-5472-8e55-0c2af00b581a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1f/1f92f42246d98eaa49b0acd36607de58a64bc4aff5e79487a175ecbf5d4ddcd8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7564118-cc79-52d5-895d-26764e2194cb" :answer/id #uuid "b7564118-cc79-52d5-895d-26764e2194cb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/71/7118cc00a570a15dd1cc4912f50705c7b3f27b231db041062b0670f7cd7bcb09.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" :answer/id #uuid "ea91dcd2-b8e4-540e-a727-c7877e69d4bf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/96/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f56a479-bb17-5e09-be93-fcf9d34e974c" :answer/id #uuid "8f56a479-bb17-5e09-be93-fcf9d34e974c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/da/da0643022b84affea31bd3f8dd8e7b3905b4fb908df46b4160ad24cce2499291.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c0c91e8-a70d-5113-bcb2-8f7a22529a09" :question/id #uuid "2c0c91e8-a70d-5113-bcb2-8f7a22529a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-79414" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f03e226c0d65852c48ef5136131db98b563da6885cb9930f6227e7908aba584.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈0.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(0)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈0$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b51f657-f4fd-5037-87ae-5da0475c5c99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b51f657-f4fd-5037-87ae-5da0475c5c99" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b51f657-f4fd-5037-87ae-5da0475c5c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18908474-09f9-51ca-b5a0-e606b75d57cf" "answer-b510f5a7-36df-5f30-a9d5-ef5382cd43a5" "answer-6d4912d0-4210-5162-87be-483c9f8d8900" "answer-0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" "answer-548b733a-19c3-5703-9791-06c6b0d56ea6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18908474-09f9-51ca-b5a0-e606b75d57cf" :answer/id #uuid "18908474-09f9-51ca-b5a0-e606b75d57cf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1b/1bf02a93f611bece0e692ee238fa3d98769f22e178ffd9c1dfec7b7ab5f57ea4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b510f5a7-36df-5f30-a9d5-ef5382cd43a5" :answer/id #uuid "b510f5a7-36df-5f30-a9d5-ef5382cd43a5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5d/5d6382c1736d5a2840a1db8f54e3c0ba9b39adc83ce48c61f30eac2fb1d4607e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d4912d0-4210-5162-87be-483c9f8d8900" :answer/id #uuid "6d4912d0-4210-5162-87be-483c9f8d8900" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7d/7d4d71df487ab7302fbd485258c1b9431f3129aaf01df10f7b9c3f274f32e6b9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" :answer/id #uuid "0d9f20fb-fdb0-549d-b771-5508dba3311e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-548b733a-19c3-5703-9791-06c6b0d56ea6" :answer/id #uuid "548b733a-19c3-5703-9791-06c6b0d56ea6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c0/c0324d643e6f82390153e4991a9cdfd3391284778e7d9718dcb2a6b3d6572f08.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2c85a10-a5e4-538c-9cb8-85994d6879ad" :question/id #uuid "e2c85a10-a5e4-538c-9cb8-85994d6879ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114754" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdf32d536487a7927752daa6ea293244ca217f528881630827bc85d6db5044d2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the critical points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the critical points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has an inflection point at $x≈2.$ At the inflection point, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈2.$\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${g}^{′}(2)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangent to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈2$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below. \n\n![](images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b46c5d02-2ff2-520b-818a-fba2dd2a7ca2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b46c5d02-2ff2-520b-818a-fba2dd2a7ca2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b46c5d02-2ff2-520b-818a-fba2dd2a7ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27e8d76b-3ba8-547f-a653-d5044bad5019" "answer-a5180969-fdc1-5e5f-a0f6-9864b26cdc4d" "answer-e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" "answer-693905f1-da25-590a-b87e-28d3be5fa75d" "answer-a2616917-5ff5-518e-8534-b133d986511c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27e8d76b-3ba8-547f-a653-d5044bad5019" :answer/id #uuid "27e8d76b-3ba8-547f-a653-d5044bad5019" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/55/5522d27e0e17ab88e39c9559581f0ba272ee8ceef9c969466bb73505e9c3bf26.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5180969-fdc1-5e5f-a0f6-9864b26cdc4d" :answer/id #uuid "a5180969-fdc1-5e5f-a0f6-9864b26cdc4d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/73/73f02c15c2bb60918b4dfdea5f7f33a86101393b9f58e64df800b2887c67aa2e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" :answer/id #uuid "e528abc0-6090-578f-a779-379c3d3c17ad" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c4/c4ffc73403729de6b9869bb70f1b466a3d426a615615e0f80d15293e97320c6b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693905f1-da25-590a-b87e-28d3be5fa75d" :answer/id #uuid "693905f1-da25-590a-b87e-28d3be5fa75d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e5/e5214bcdbcd4d596234d2e9900d35fc1a43b4363f8162abe3df6a42d1f1b7bde.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2616917-5ff5-518e-8534-b133d986511c" :answer/id #uuid "a2616917-5ff5-518e-8534-b133d986511c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f6/f6c4aca7259e3fe0999202dfcf0e572238b9bfd8263699768c65df89daafc8aa.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-60da6948-77d4-526b-84a9-f5ff89c1d7b4" :question/id #uuid "60da6948-77d4-526b-84a9-f5ff89c1d7b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114769" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c51543f8cf91203bd242fd62fec82936788b54490466c2af25c995f7a25af8cf.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈0.5$ and $x≈1.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈0$ and $x≈2.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(1.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈0.5$ and $x≈1.5$ are  negative and positive, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/42/4289eb05ab23d48f2727848724d3cd2f130cdb5fe9d92d569f3abd05b534c0e2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57227157-500d-5351-8239-220e276379f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57227157-500d-5351-8239-220e276379f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "57227157-500d-5351-8239-220e276379f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" "answer-6997daeb-c0f2-57ac-a20c-08939ec9a4ad" "answer-131edabd-fe45-5f12-a92e-17e4a00daca6" "answer-6b31e19d-bc9f-5077-a168-b009d12bcc44" "answer-64e53d3a-08ff-5a45-8e4f-5b7915cc9e97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" :answer/id #uuid "dc0a17be-f006-5693-bab4-bcdbb6905ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/42/4289eb05ab23d48f2727848724d3cd2f130cdb5fe9d92d569f3abd05b534c0e2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6997daeb-c0f2-57ac-a20c-08939ec9a4ad" :answer/id #uuid "6997daeb-c0f2-57ac-a20c-08939ec9a4ad" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/72/7299c08ee83650af2291aecbacdf40a3c142141bfbb1109afa07cb4269a00fce.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-131edabd-fe45-5f12-a92e-17e4a00daca6" :answer/id #uuid "131edabd-fe45-5f12-a92e-17e4a00daca6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/85/856eb2e47ccb4aada8bb3be5c44f2c1b6bbee298b607946d7694cc259e4f7786.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b31e19d-bc9f-5077-a168-b009d12bcc44" :answer/id #uuid "6b31e19d-bc9f-5077-a168-b009d12bcc44" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/99/9902d1174794b89cc6ed4f4c002cead71e4625a4bc3bcb1d04881e1903882fde.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64e53d3a-08ff-5a45-8e4f-5b7915cc9e97" :answer/id #uuid "64e53d3a-08ff-5a45-8e4f-5b7915cc9e97" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e9/e9aeba3b197425ddf7f98e36096be174f54f76c6c1ac5aea453c63de72e01a3d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0028aa3d-01ab-5dd0-b507-a0e026d36abf" :question/id #uuid "0028aa3d-01ab-5dd0-b507-a0e026d36abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79422" :question/problem "![](images/74/74a33fb06f8ef0f2f9f7935f5c53209392f3289e20be803b9d82737a46b31c06.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave down in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-945133ec-d03d-5f9a-a393-2a2fb159bf47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-945133ec-d03d-5f9a-a393-2a2fb159bf47" :answer-field/id #uuid "945133ec-d03d-5f9a-a393-2a2fb159bf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d442c6ea-c924-5f33-ba89-5f1580048fe6" "answer-f3e63383-4a56-5400-a091-38b716d1f0cc" "answer-979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" "answer-8761dacd-e9a3-5e7e-a6c3-02a68914b77e" "answer-3c82e3fc-15f7-5054-be9d-ba1e88ee764a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d442c6ea-c924-5f33-ba89-5f1580048fe6" :answer/id #uuid "d442c6ea-c924-5f33-ba89-5f1580048fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/24/246f2bf42fb02412bb06fbec262e2c30abcb0286f5419ff51b207e43acff6736.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3e63383-4a56-5400-a091-38b716d1f0cc" :answer/id #uuid "f3e63383-4a56-5400-a091-38b716d1f0cc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2d/2dcf5fe5acbaa9ed150dd31e76390f50288165b68b1c7d9f77a340222bb0f6a7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" :answer/id #uuid "979f0cc0-6520-56fc-81a4-dda171bee116" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c1/c16ffbba0310799c36ee1f21359b96f93f73ad121b7d5770784fbf6033ca0446.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8761dacd-e9a3-5e7e-a6c3-02a68914b77e" :answer/id #uuid "8761dacd-e9a3-5e7e-a6c3-02a68914b77e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ce/ce7e8b147cc2fa4eb7959787d10b0ea635e5ac6411cdb0df9626a410d7d0589f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c82e3fc-15f7-5054-be9d-ba1e88ee764a" :answer/id #uuid "3c82e3fc-15f7-5054-be9d-ba1e88ee764a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d7/d7d32a47a04bca11ba9321256de9c23ce24a7e79bd4495c12028b658602d88de.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4fa7b9a4-0348-582a-97ef-22ec70d27b82" :question/id #uuid "4fa7b9a4-0348-582a-97ef-22ec70d27b82" :question/math-academy-id "q-114743" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2a62b8616e1178cc7200c91e4e45366b5aece9d7e9838f02b9c60217fd79f8b.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-760b0e18-9ea3-5b0b-8bc6-06ccc419b799"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-760b0e18-9ea3-5b0b-8bc6-06ccc419b799" :answer-field/id #uuid "760b0e18-9ea3-5b0b-8bc6-06ccc419b799" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" "answer-00e44565-9c53-5bb8-9cc9-7639994a6273" "answer-7f071171-1572-5433-8c60-46d61cf139aa" "answer-ba57dcc5-0a7f-5a7a-8029-90e9d6d4094e" "answer-4b47436b-6b10-5b3c-bd03-8dfc22297805"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" :answer/id #uuid "fb74d49e-0382-5626-bafa-cc9547fa60bc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2d/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e44565-9c53-5bb8-9cc9-7639994a6273" :answer/id #uuid "00e44565-9c53-5bb8-9cc9-7639994a6273" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2d/2dc2c5bc8ca4e173a3ee4b853aa85666b2761cebedfdb8ae7038b794ce86af1c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f071171-1572-5433-8c60-46d61cf139aa" :answer/id #uuid "7f071171-1572-5433-8c60-46d61cf139aa" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4f/4f0e941cff224d51f4b92ce4913c53f0b9e4346e7a8a5382d0259637da6321a9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba57dcc5-0a7f-5a7a-8029-90e9d6d4094e" :answer/id #uuid "ba57dcc5-0a7f-5a7a-8029-90e9d6d4094e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b6/b6f488650c668f015b88651a7ae9604b1d56bca6c268b90e4c5db96d406cf4d4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b47436b-6b10-5b3c-bd03-8dfc22297805" :answer/id #uuid "4b47436b-6b10-5b3c-bd03-8dfc22297805" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d1/d1ff02104c55267fc08b7721707af650ded99120109acad65e95c00861c90e69.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102004 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x),$ as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and an inflection point at $x=0.5.$\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)?$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5.$ So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101061 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
